WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:08.420
KARIM LAKHANI: Noubar Afeyan, thank
you very much for joining our course.

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:12.260
Please tell us a bit more about
yourself and your role as CEO

00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:14.517
and founder of Flagship Pioneering.

00:00:14.517 --> 00:00:15.350
NOUBAR AFEYAN: Sure.

00:00:15.350 --> 00:00:18.050
Well thanks for inviting me.

00:00:18.050 --> 00:00:23.360
So I've been involved in
innovation and entrepreneurship

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:25.790
in the life sciences for about 31 years.

00:00:25.790 --> 00:00:30.740
I did a PhD in what was then a
new field by chemical engineering.

00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:35.410
And since then have found
different ways of deriving value

00:00:35.410 --> 00:00:38.360
from making breakthroughs in that area.

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:43.070
And initially, I followed
the kind of traditional route

00:00:43.070 --> 00:00:47.240
of developing technologies,
raising capital, and developing

00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:49.790
startup companies, venture
backed startup companies.

00:00:49.790 --> 00:00:53.600
And then, more recently what Flagship
Pioneering really specializes in

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:59.870
is in a different way of thinking about
both innovation and entrepreneurship.

00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:03.290
And we are looking to systematize
the process and make it something

00:01:03.290 --> 00:01:07.365
that an institution can do
instead of a group of individuals.

00:01:07.365 --> 00:01:08.240
KARIM LAKHANI: Great.

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:14.040
So tell me first how you got the bug of
taking technologies and bringing them

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:14.540
to market.

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:16.755
What was the spark that got you into--

00:01:16.755 --> 00:01:20.280
NOUBAR AFEYAN: Well different people
are inspired by different things.

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:22.730
In my case, it was
actually quite memorable.

00:01:22.730 --> 00:01:28.220
I had no intention or no idea
in 1985 graduate students

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:32.120
let alone kind of not from this country
didn't exactly think that they were

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:33.620
going to go off and start a company.

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:37.470
That's a pretty recent phenomenon
in the last 15 years or so.

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:41.990
But so I was a graduate student working
in a pretty esoteric kind of project.

00:01:41.990 --> 00:01:47.670
And I happened to find myself at an
NSF conference that MIT had sent me to.

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:49.910
You have to remember,
in the 1980s the US was

00:01:49.910 --> 00:01:52.910
worried that it was going to lose
its competitiveness against Japan.

00:01:52.910 --> 00:01:54.950
And there were all these
kind of meetings about,

00:01:54.950 --> 00:01:56.575
how are we going to compete with Japan?

00:01:56.575 --> 00:01:57.110
Et cetera.

00:01:57.110 --> 00:02:00.570
And they must have needed somebody
who knew how to spell biotechnology.

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:01.910
So they sent me down.

00:02:01.910 --> 00:02:05.570
And at this conference, which
was otherwise utterly boring,

00:02:05.570 --> 00:02:07.400
I happened at lunch
to sit with somebody.

00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:12.390
And I got enough courage to ask him
what he did and who he was and stuff.

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:15.980
And he started telling me how
he had started a company some 35

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:20.070
years earlier with another friend of his
and they were a new breed of engineer.

00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:21.440
This was electronic engineer.

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:23.255
They came out of the power industry.

00:02:23.255 --> 00:02:24.630
And I was listening to his story.

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:28.160
And this is kind of I
had nothing to relate to,

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:29.960
except when he said
new breed of engineer.

00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:31.010
I kind of thought, well,
that's interesting.

00:02:31.010 --> 00:02:32.177
I'm a new breed of engineer.

00:02:32.177 --> 00:02:35.240
And engineers had not done
biology ever in the past.

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:37.550
And he said they didn't know
what these engineers would

00:02:37.550 --> 00:02:40.500
make but they knew they would need
to measure whatever they made.

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:43.310
And so, he and his friend
of his started this effort

00:02:43.310 --> 00:02:45.440
to make an oscilloscope in their garage.

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:48.185
And they started selling
it to their friends.

00:02:48.185 --> 00:02:51.050
And then, that led them to
make even more products.

00:02:51.050 --> 00:02:52.530
And so it's kind of interesting.

00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:53.690
I had no idea who he was.

00:02:53.690 --> 00:02:58.633
And but it was interesting to relate
to I was a new breed of engineer.

00:02:58.633 --> 00:03:00.800
We didn't know what biological
engineers would make.

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:03.425
But we did know that they were
going to need to measure things.

00:03:03.425 --> 00:03:07.670
And so, probably out of a
deficiency of fairly linear thinking

00:03:07.670 --> 00:03:10.210
as engineers sometimes have,
I was just mapping it to me.

00:03:10.210 --> 00:03:11.960
And I was thinking,
oh that's interesting.

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:15.230
And then I asked him who he was and it
was David Packard from Hewlett Packard.

00:03:15.230 --> 00:03:20.258
And he spent the next couple of hours
telling me just one on one just out of,

00:03:20.258 --> 00:03:22.550
I could have been sitting on
a park bench with the guy.

00:03:22.550 --> 00:03:24.590
I mean he was literally
just very relatable.

00:03:24.590 --> 00:03:27.812
And literally I came back to
MIT to my graduate kind of lab.

00:03:27.812 --> 00:03:30.770
And then, I thought, well, how am I
going to figure out how to do this?

00:03:30.770 --> 00:03:32.660
I really wanted to do this.

00:03:32.660 --> 00:03:36.260
And I went to Sloan School
and said, can I take courses?

00:03:36.260 --> 00:03:39.440
They had absolutely no courses
on entrepreneurship at the time.

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:41.420
Now, everybody has 1,000 courses.

00:03:41.420 --> 00:03:43.280
But it was not something
that was taught.

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:47.030
So that's when I actually started
finding my way to Eric von Hippel's

00:03:47.030 --> 00:03:50.300
class and to several other people's
class who were teaching management

00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:53.940
of technology, innovation, there
was a corporate venture capital

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:54.590
course I took.

00:03:54.590 --> 00:03:55.447
I don't know why.

00:03:55.447 --> 00:03:57.530
All to just kind of get
myself introduced to that.

00:03:57.530 --> 00:03:59.120
And that's kind of how I started.

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:00.590
KARIM LAKHANI: That's incredible.

00:04:00.590 --> 00:04:02.973
So David Packard was the
source of your inspiration.

00:04:02.973 --> 00:04:03.890
NOUBAR AFEYAN: It was.

00:04:03.890 --> 00:04:04.580
Randomly.

00:04:04.580 --> 00:04:05.675
Randomly.

00:04:05.675 --> 00:04:07.795
Because he was relatable.

00:04:07.795 --> 00:04:10.670
It's kind of one of the things I've
learned over all these years I've

00:04:10.670 --> 00:04:15.830
done this is that sometimes people make
entrepreneurship some kind of almost

00:04:15.830 --> 00:04:20.410
like art or science or there's
almost celebrity associated with it.

00:04:20.410 --> 00:04:23.480
And I always resent that
because it makes it unrelatable.

00:04:23.480 --> 00:04:24.770
It makes it look like--

00:04:24.770 --> 00:04:25.950
I like playing basketball.

00:04:25.950 --> 00:04:29.255
But when I look at Michael Jordan
I realize I'm not going to be him.

00:04:29.255 --> 00:04:33.110
And those kinds of celebrity
figures don't encourage the sport.

00:04:33.110 --> 00:04:34.910
They actually discourage the sport.

00:04:34.910 --> 00:04:37.993
I mean, without wanting because you're
saying, I'm never going to be that.

00:04:37.993 --> 00:04:40.700
Well he was a guy who was clearly
a celebrity in doing that.

00:04:40.700 --> 00:04:42.650
But he was absolutely no different.

00:04:42.650 --> 00:04:47.000
And what he impressed upon me is that if
I was willing to kind of stick with it

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:50.360
and iterate and learn and adapt, he
didn't give me any other tidbits.

00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:51.810
There wasn't that much to give.

00:04:51.810 --> 00:04:54.200
So I think, and then since
then, part of the reason

00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:56.480
I teach, and I've taught
for the last 17 years

00:04:56.480 --> 00:04:59.300
various aspects of entrepreneurship
innovation is just that.

00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:01.550
Because I realized the
more people realize

00:05:01.550 --> 00:05:08.520
that this is not some kind of highbrowed
activity, the better the economy is.

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:14.430
KARIM LAKHANI: So tell me from that
inspiration to your first startup.

00:05:14.430 --> 00:05:18.780
NOUBAR AFEYAN: In 1987, which was a
couple of years after this meeting,

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:21.660
after I had kind of exposed myself
a little bit to what innovation

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:26.790
and technology was, and without really
an idea to start a company around,

00:05:26.790 --> 00:05:29.350
as I was getting close to
graduating I started thinking,

00:05:29.350 --> 00:05:33.720
well I should really keep that
as an alternative in mind.

00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:37.710
I went out and met a startup company
that was then just forming out

00:05:37.710 --> 00:05:40.560
in the West Coast, realized
that being employed in one

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:44.447
wasn't kind of the same thing for me
because I realized other people were

00:05:44.447 --> 00:05:47.530
trying to figure out what needed to
be done and I was going to execute it.

00:05:47.530 --> 00:05:49.363
And while that was
interesting that I didn't

00:05:49.363 --> 00:05:51.910
consider that to be the same thing.

00:05:51.910 --> 00:05:55.120
And so, I started I started kind
of trying to figure out, OK.

00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:57.990
If I was going to do this,
what would I do it with?

00:05:57.990 --> 00:06:00.835
And pretty linearly, I thought, OK.

00:06:00.835 --> 00:06:03.960
What about making instruments for the
biotech industry just like these guys

00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:06.990
have instruments for the
electronics industry?

00:06:06.990 --> 00:06:09.300
And I started looking at, OK.

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:10.330
Well what the needs are?

00:06:10.330 --> 00:06:13.890
So it gets kind of pretty kind
of classical way of thinking

00:06:13.890 --> 00:06:18.297
about unmet needs first,
which again for a technologist

00:06:18.297 --> 00:06:20.130
wasn't the first thought
that comes to mind.

00:06:20.130 --> 00:06:23.760
But the folks at Sloan
impressed upon me that far more

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:27.300
interesting than finding
customers for what I could do

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:29.610
was finding problems
that were worth solving.

00:06:29.610 --> 00:06:32.430
And so, I looked at a
bunch of different things

00:06:32.430 --> 00:06:35.640
where we could apply engineering
mindset problem solving.

00:06:35.640 --> 00:06:38.730
And there were many because
the field is so new.

00:06:38.730 --> 00:06:43.020
I always find that if you're working
in a field that's rapidly changing,

00:06:43.020 --> 00:06:48.000
there's an innate advantage
that if you're a newcomer

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:51.660
there isn't that much difference
between you and the current players

00:06:51.660 --> 00:06:54.600
because they also haven't
been at it that long.

00:06:54.600 --> 00:06:56.700
So that's how I started.

00:06:56.700 --> 00:07:01.920
So I basically wrote a business
plan as I was graduating from MIT.

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:03.960
My first meeting with
a venture capitalist

00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:07.320
was, I think it was something like
October 20, 30, or something like that.

00:07:07.320 --> 00:07:10.622
29th, I forget the exact date in
1987, which I remember very well.

00:07:10.622 --> 00:07:13.830
It was a Tuesday because it was the day
right after the stock market crashed.

00:07:13.830 --> 00:07:16.612
So the Black Monday that that occurred--

00:07:16.612 --> 00:07:18.070
KARIM LAKHANI: That was October 19.

00:07:18.070 --> 00:07:18.760
NOUBAR AFEYAN: October 19.

00:07:18.760 --> 00:07:19.950
So it was the day after.

00:07:19.950 --> 00:07:22.188
October 20.

00:07:22.188 --> 00:07:24.660
And I remember having
set up a meeting going

00:07:24.660 --> 00:07:29.250
to see a at the time famous venture
capitalist Ginger Moore at Oak Ventures

00:07:29.250 --> 00:07:32.670
who in the life sciences area was
one of very few people who invested.

00:07:32.670 --> 00:07:33.865
And yeah.

00:07:33.865 --> 00:07:36.240
I went to see her and it was
kind of quite a stark memory

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:39.960
because I was 24 years old
and I had the business plan

00:07:39.960 --> 00:07:41.640
and I didn't know what I was doing.

00:07:41.640 --> 00:07:45.090
And here was this person who had
done this for 20 years already.

00:07:45.090 --> 00:07:50.160
And she was completely
frazzled, lost, kind of

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:51.600
devastated at what was going on.

00:07:51.600 --> 00:07:53.220
And I thought, boy.

00:07:53.220 --> 00:07:56.040
It's a weird introduction to the field.

00:07:56.040 --> 00:08:00.330
It was the best thing that happened
to me because frankly, 31 years later,

00:08:00.330 --> 00:08:03.330
even in the last few days,
the feeling is quite similar.

00:08:03.330 --> 00:08:07.290
And so, one thing I learned back
then is that if you have a calm day

00:08:07.290 --> 00:08:10.080
it's actually out of character.

00:08:10.080 --> 00:08:12.030
But it's usually just very turbulent.

00:08:12.030 --> 00:08:13.710
But anyway, and it also tested me.

00:08:13.710 --> 00:08:17.282
Because her advice to me was,
you look like you can do this.

00:08:17.282 --> 00:08:18.240
You've got a good idea.

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:20.490
But you're never going to be able
to raise money for the next year.

00:08:20.490 --> 00:08:21.565
Go do something else.

00:08:21.565 --> 00:08:23.190
Because the market had been devastated.

00:08:23.190 --> 00:08:24.560
KARIM LAKHANI: Interesting.

00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:25.470
NOUBAR AFEYAN: And it was interesting.

00:08:25.470 --> 00:08:26.790
And so, that forced me to--

00:08:26.790 --> 00:08:28.260
I didn't take her advice.

00:08:28.260 --> 00:08:30.930
But at the same time I did realize
it was going to take a while

00:08:30.930 --> 00:08:32.350
to be able to raise money.

00:08:32.350 --> 00:08:34.250
And so, I started trying to--

00:08:34.250 --> 00:08:39.150
I got a side job at MIT doing
technology licensing for a year.

00:08:39.150 --> 00:08:41.580
My professor was generous to
kind of they needed somebody

00:08:41.580 --> 00:08:43.049
to do some licensing work.

00:08:43.049 --> 00:08:46.860
And I'd continued to develop the
business plan and got a lot of help

00:08:46.860 --> 00:08:52.020
from Ed Roberts who's a kind of quite
a legendary figure in entrepreneurship.

00:08:52.020 --> 00:08:56.370
And about, let's say maybe
almost a year later, I

00:08:56.370 --> 00:09:01.980
ended up getting some seed capital
from quite unlikely sources,

00:09:01.980 --> 00:09:03.960
meaning just random people I had met.

00:09:03.960 --> 00:09:06.600
100 different people.

00:09:06.600 --> 00:09:10.110
I used to think, and this may
be useful for your audience,

00:09:10.110 --> 00:09:13.615
at the time I used to think that
if I'm just successful ultimately--

00:09:13.615 --> 00:09:14.490
I felt like a beggar.

00:09:14.490 --> 00:09:16.198
I mean that's kind of
what it feels like.

00:09:16.198 --> 00:09:17.380
I had absolutely nothing.

00:09:17.380 --> 00:09:18.630
I had no technology.

00:09:18.630 --> 00:09:19.647
I had a piece of paper.

00:09:19.647 --> 00:09:20.480
I had my background.

00:09:20.480 --> 00:09:21.300
KARIM LAKHANI: Just the idea.

00:09:21.300 --> 00:09:22.508
NOUBAR AFEYAN: Just the idea.

00:09:22.508 --> 00:09:26.460
There was no proof of concept, there was
no technology I was licensing, nothing.

00:09:26.460 --> 00:09:27.430
I had an idea.

00:09:27.430 --> 00:09:28.830
I had filed a patent.

00:09:28.830 --> 00:09:31.770
And it was the closest thing to begging.

00:09:31.770 --> 00:09:33.120
They have it, you don't.

00:09:33.120 --> 00:09:35.770
You need it, they may
not need it as much.

00:09:35.770 --> 00:09:39.240
And the chance that I'm going to give
you [INAUDIBLE] back is pretty dubious.

00:09:39.240 --> 00:09:39.993
A similar act.

00:09:39.993 --> 00:09:41.910
And I was telling myself,
if you're successful

00:09:41.910 --> 00:09:43.530
then you don't have to beg anymore.

00:09:43.530 --> 00:09:45.900
And 31 years later I
realized that if you're

00:09:45.900 --> 00:09:47.910
successful you beg for more and more.

00:09:47.910 --> 00:09:51.360
But that feeling doesn't go
away because any time you're

00:09:51.360 --> 00:09:57.930
trying to kind of pre-sell the
value promise of innovation that

00:09:57.930 --> 00:10:03.360
isn't proven, and then eventually
realize it and reward people,

00:10:03.360 --> 00:10:05.710
it's an extreme form of fundraising.

00:10:05.710 --> 00:10:08.810
And that's kind of what
comes with the territory.

00:10:08.810 --> 00:10:16.300
So I ended up getting some seed capital
and started this company which ended up

00:10:16.300 --> 00:10:17.860
growing over some period of time.

00:10:17.860 --> 00:10:20.635
By '89, we raised some
venture capital money.

00:10:20.635 --> 00:10:23.260
This was, again, the old days of
where venture capitalists used

00:10:23.260 --> 00:10:26.970
to put $100,000, $300,000 in.

00:10:26.970 --> 00:10:31.240
And then, at the most a
million, $2 million in ever.

00:10:31.240 --> 00:10:32.380
There was not seed capital.

00:10:32.380 --> 00:10:35.080
Seed capital back then was $25,000.

00:10:35.080 --> 00:10:38.980
So at the end, this company called
Perceptive Biosystems ended up

00:10:38.980 --> 00:10:42.670
raising $11 million from
seven venture capital

00:10:42.670 --> 00:10:46.810
firms of which some
venerable firms like Venrock,

00:10:46.810 --> 00:10:51.040
and Bessemer, and Highland
Capital were the big investors.

00:10:51.040 --> 00:10:53.412
And 3i, the Bank of
England, I mean it was crazy

00:10:53.412 --> 00:10:55.870
that they were all putting in
a half a million, $1 million.

00:10:55.870 --> 00:10:58.120
And ultimately, the
company actually took off.

00:10:58.120 --> 00:10:59.920
In '92 we went public.

00:10:59.920 --> 00:11:02.545
In '97, we had about
$110 million in revenue.

00:11:02.545 --> 00:11:08.920
So we became a very established
player and leader in the protein

00:11:08.920 --> 00:11:10.570
analytical instrument field.

00:11:10.570 --> 00:11:12.235
And so, that was that journey.

00:11:12.235 --> 00:11:17.440
It was every day was as
exhilarating as it was terrifying.

00:11:17.440 --> 00:11:20.680
And now, this is a well trodden road.

00:11:20.680 --> 00:11:26.155
But back then, I remember very clearly
the most recent company that Inc. 500--

00:11:26.155 --> 00:11:28.030
we were around the Inc.
500 pretty early on--

00:11:28.030 --> 00:11:30.025
had featured was Lotus.

00:11:30.025 --> 00:11:35.950
And of course lotus had subsequently
kind of been sold and disbanded.

00:11:35.950 --> 00:11:37.780
And before that, it was digital.

00:11:37.780 --> 00:11:40.750
So it wasn't like there were
all that many companies to say,

00:11:40.750 --> 00:11:41.890
I want to do that.

00:11:41.890 --> 00:11:43.367
You had to figure it out yourself.

00:11:43.367 --> 00:11:44.200
KARIM LAKHANI: Yeah.

00:11:44.200 --> 00:11:46.180
That's an incredible story.

00:11:46.180 --> 00:11:51.490
So you went on this journey, you
have an idea, you incubate the idea,

00:11:51.490 --> 00:11:57.670
you persist, form a team, you
raise capital, you go public,

00:11:57.670 --> 00:11:59.790
then you're back for more.

00:11:59.790 --> 00:12:01.660
So it's not one and done for you.

00:12:01.660 --> 00:12:04.070
This has become a lifetime.

00:12:04.070 --> 00:12:06.560
This is your-- so tell
us this transformation.

00:12:06.560 --> 00:12:10.337
So what that was and what caused
you to sort of think about this.

00:12:10.337 --> 00:12:11.170
NOUBAR AFEYAN: Sure.

00:12:11.170 --> 00:12:13.630
Well so first, I'll go back
to the Perceptive thing

00:12:13.630 --> 00:12:19.150
because one of the things you
realize is that I ended up wanting--

00:12:19.150 --> 00:12:20.950
I mean I did this
largely because I wanted

00:12:20.950 --> 00:12:25.015
to learn how to do it as opposed
to the rewards of doing it

00:12:25.015 --> 00:12:26.110
or a lot of other things.

00:12:26.110 --> 00:12:30.400
I just thought it was so
unlikely that I could pick up

00:12:30.400 --> 00:12:34.500
the minimum needed to be
able to succeed minimally

00:12:34.500 --> 00:12:37.510
that that was a real adventure.

00:12:37.510 --> 00:12:41.378
So I wasn't a good manager, and
probably still not a good manager,

00:12:41.378 --> 00:12:44.170
I didn't know how to raise money,
I didn't know how to hire people,

00:12:44.170 --> 00:12:46.270
I knew even less about
how to fire people.

00:12:46.270 --> 00:12:49.420
Excruciatingly painful
to do for the first time.

00:12:49.420 --> 00:12:52.600
I had hired a bunch of
experienced people early on.

00:12:52.600 --> 00:12:55.570
The one thing I learned
that I think is quite kind

00:12:55.570 --> 00:12:58.720
of ageless in terms of a
lesson, I use it to this day,

00:12:58.720 --> 00:13:03.340
is that especially when you're young and
when you're not expected to know much,

00:13:03.340 --> 00:13:07.460
the fact that you actually use that
to ingratiate yourself to people

00:13:07.460 --> 00:13:11.020
so that they help you in
helping you figure out something

00:13:11.020 --> 00:13:16.210
is a really powerful source
of kind of borrowed intellect.

00:13:16.210 --> 00:13:20.620
I mean you just you cannot
have wisdom early on.

00:13:20.620 --> 00:13:24.338
The next best thing is to admit it
and to let other people give you

00:13:24.338 --> 00:13:24.880
their wisdom.

00:13:24.880 --> 00:13:27.340
And by this I don't mean
advisors and mentors.

00:13:27.340 --> 00:13:29.792
I just mean people who just
kind of like you and decide

00:13:29.792 --> 00:13:31.250
that they want to help you succeed.

00:13:31.250 --> 00:13:33.580
There's a lot of people
out there who as long

00:13:33.580 --> 00:13:35.950
as you give them this
permission marketing,

00:13:35.950 --> 00:13:37.420
this is like permission advice.

00:13:37.420 --> 00:13:41.075
If you give them the permission to give
you advice and they see you use it,

00:13:41.075 --> 00:13:41.950
not that they go, OK.

00:13:41.950 --> 00:13:43.367
I'll take that into consideration.

00:13:43.367 --> 00:13:48.897
But you truly kind of let
go of pretending that a lot.

00:13:48.897 --> 00:13:50.980
And I didn't have this
problem because I literally

00:13:50.980 --> 00:13:52.760
didn't know a lot about anything.

00:13:52.760 --> 00:13:56.570
My PhD had nothing to do with what the
company did, and I knew the language,

00:13:56.570 --> 00:13:57.155
but not much.

00:13:57.155 --> 00:13:58.030
I had no connections.

00:13:58.030 --> 00:13:59.415
I was an immigrant.

00:13:59.415 --> 00:14:04.600
I came originally from Lebanon,
escaped the war, went to Canada,

00:14:04.600 --> 00:14:06.380
kind of grew up in a foreign place.

00:14:06.380 --> 00:14:08.840
So I had zero expectations.

00:14:08.840 --> 00:14:11.110
Zero entitlement.

00:14:11.110 --> 00:14:12.910
I was not even American, I was Canadian.

00:14:12.910 --> 00:14:15.820
There was a lot of immigration
issues back then for different kind.

00:14:15.820 --> 00:14:17.320
It was very hard to get papers here.

00:14:17.320 --> 00:14:19.790
There was no-- H1
visas were a new thing.

00:14:19.790 --> 00:14:23.140
And so, it's interesting in
hindsight when you look at it.

00:14:23.140 --> 00:14:30.760
But anyway, so learning how to manage,
how to create business strategy,

00:14:30.760 --> 00:14:34.390
I never-- to this day
I don't quite know what

00:14:34.390 --> 00:14:37.480
strategy is when it comes
to a startup because you

00:14:37.480 --> 00:14:41.780
have 100 different things you could do
and 100 different ways of doing them.

00:14:41.780 --> 00:14:44.110
And choosing one I suppose is strategy.

00:14:44.110 --> 00:14:45.535
KARIM LAKHANI: Yes.

00:14:45.535 --> 00:14:46.735
Strategy is about choice.

00:14:46.735 --> 00:14:48.110
NOUBAR AFEYAN: It's about choice.

00:14:48.110 --> 00:14:51.070
But in this case, there's nothing else.

00:14:51.070 --> 00:14:54.700
It's a bit of an arbitrary choice
in the beginning, uninformed choice,

00:14:54.700 --> 00:14:56.268
followed by lots of iteration.

00:14:56.268 --> 00:14:57.310
That's what I've learned.

00:14:57.310 --> 00:15:00.190
Anyway, so and but
before getting to kind

00:15:00.190 --> 00:15:03.550
of what I do now, what ended
up happening is in '94 or so,

00:15:03.550 --> 00:15:06.880
this company, I had iterated
enough and made enough mistakes

00:15:06.880 --> 00:15:08.980
that I had a pretty good team.

00:15:08.980 --> 00:15:13.330
The revenues were growing,
we were expanding.

00:15:13.330 --> 00:15:17.140
And for the first time I
started realizing that, OK.

00:15:17.140 --> 00:15:18.880
I kind of sort of get it.

00:15:18.880 --> 00:15:23.280
And I became interested in
innovation and value creation again.

00:15:23.280 --> 00:15:25.030
And I realized that
in this company, which

00:15:25.030 --> 00:15:28.060
was by that time about 400
people, it's really hard

00:15:28.060 --> 00:15:31.630
to get rewarded for innovation
because even though we were little,

00:15:31.630 --> 00:15:32.790
we were already too big.

00:15:32.790 --> 00:15:33.370
KARIM LAKHANI: You
were already focused--

00:15:33.370 --> 00:15:34.420
NOUBAR AFEYAN: For that.

00:15:34.420 --> 00:15:35.920
We had a line of products.

00:15:35.920 --> 00:15:38.150
And there's so much
more we could have done.

00:15:38.150 --> 00:15:43.600
So I started actually experimenting
with innovating with other people

00:15:43.600 --> 00:15:45.490
and helping other
people start companies.

00:15:45.490 --> 00:15:49.030
So I was still running this first
company as CEO of a public company,

00:15:49.030 --> 00:15:53.350
but I was able to, and when my board
knew it and were in support of it,

00:15:53.350 --> 00:15:56.080
I was able to get involved
in a few other companies

00:15:56.080 --> 00:16:00.130
that where people were either
they had a notion or an idea.

00:16:00.130 --> 00:16:02.120
But by now I was a little experienced.

00:16:02.120 --> 00:16:05.670
And so, ironically they were
looking for my experience.

00:16:05.670 --> 00:16:08.560
And here I was still winging it.

00:16:08.560 --> 00:16:11.650
And so, in the '90s kind
of between '94 and '97

00:16:11.650 --> 00:16:16.300
I got involved in about four other
roles as a co-founder of companies.

00:16:16.300 --> 00:16:21.525
One was a drug discovery company
that I ended up being chairman of.

00:16:21.525 --> 00:16:24.400
And then we raised capital from a
number of other venture capitalists

00:16:24.400 --> 00:16:27.820
to grow a very successful company.

00:16:27.820 --> 00:16:28.810
It was sold.

00:16:28.810 --> 00:16:33.730
We started a cancer diagnostics company,
started a cancer vaccine company.

00:16:33.730 --> 00:16:34.690
And I found--

00:16:34.690 --> 00:16:35.800
KARIM LAKHANI: This
sounds like an addiction.

00:16:35.800 --> 00:16:36.910
You were just starting companies

00:16:36.910 --> 00:16:37.700
NOUBAR AFEYAN: It was interesting.

00:16:37.700 --> 00:16:38.450
No, I'll tell you.

00:16:38.450 --> 00:16:39.558
This was interesting.

00:16:39.558 --> 00:16:40.600
I was not doing it alone.

00:16:40.600 --> 00:16:43.360
So I had gone from
founder to co-founder.

00:16:43.360 --> 00:16:46.000
And co-founder is a more
scalable role than founder.

00:16:46.000 --> 00:16:49.900
But at the same time, but I was
not an investor in these things.

00:16:49.900 --> 00:16:54.430
I hadn't taken any money
out of my first company.

00:16:54.430 --> 00:16:58.000
But I'll tell you why I got interested
in this, is that at the time

00:16:58.000 --> 00:17:03.250
the only kind of entrepreneur there was
one and done or a serial entrepreneur.

00:17:03.250 --> 00:17:07.990
And I had thought kind of skeptically
around serial entrepreneurship.

00:17:07.990 --> 00:17:09.160
I didn't know anything.

00:17:09.160 --> 00:17:12.910
But I always thought,
well, if you built a lot

00:17:12.910 --> 00:17:17.550
of value, what is there to show by
doing it again, and again, and again?

00:17:17.550 --> 00:17:19.180
A silly thought.

00:17:19.180 --> 00:17:22.060
So I thought what about
parallel entrepreneurship?

00:17:22.060 --> 00:17:24.490
And back in the '90s I used
to go around and tell people,

00:17:24.490 --> 00:17:26.050
entrepreneurship should be parallel.

00:17:26.050 --> 00:17:30.220
It's like parallel chess where people
play simultaneously multiple people.

00:17:30.220 --> 00:17:33.310
I always thought that's incredibly
hard compared to playing one game.

00:17:33.310 --> 00:17:35.695
It's hard enough to play one.

00:17:35.695 --> 00:17:40.090
And I must say that actually was
just a kind of a hypothesis that

00:17:40.090 --> 00:17:43.338
led to what I do now because
as I tried to do that,

00:17:43.338 --> 00:17:45.130
I realized that when
you're doing something

00:17:45.130 --> 00:17:50.590
in parallel that isn't as defined
as, let's say, calculus or something.

00:17:50.590 --> 00:17:52.870
It's not a discipline yet.

00:17:52.870 --> 00:17:56.140
It forces you to focus on the
essence of what you're doing

00:17:56.140 --> 00:17:59.840
and not all the idiosyncrasies
of every single case.

00:17:59.840 --> 00:18:02.360
So I started kind of
forcibly to say, OK.

00:18:02.360 --> 00:18:05.680
What can I do if I'm
spending a few hours a week

00:18:05.680 --> 00:18:09.220
on a project that in and of
itself wants to change the world,

00:18:09.220 --> 00:18:14.560
but my role in it, my involvement in it
has to be strategic, surgical, highly

00:18:14.560 --> 00:18:18.430
leveraged, but not infinite
involvement of time, which is what

00:18:18.430 --> 00:18:20.320
you do when you're doing one company.

00:18:20.320 --> 00:18:23.040
And so, those experiences kind of--

00:18:23.040 --> 00:18:25.510
So in the end, the first
company went public

00:18:25.510 --> 00:18:28.480
and it was sold five years later.

00:18:28.480 --> 00:18:30.310
Three of the other
companies went public.

00:18:30.310 --> 00:18:31.330
Two of them were sold.

00:18:31.330 --> 00:18:33.580
And this is now in '97, '98.

00:18:33.580 --> 00:18:36.520
And eventually, I kind of
started getting more intrigued

00:18:36.520 --> 00:18:37.990
by parallel entrepreneurship.

00:18:37.990 --> 00:18:40.760
And I thought I would
devote myself to now going

00:18:40.760 --> 00:18:45.130
the next step, which is to say,
if you can do this in parallel,

00:18:45.130 --> 00:18:47.200
why not do this institutionally?

00:18:47.200 --> 00:18:51.550
Because the next step there is, is
it a solo game or is it a team game?

00:18:51.550 --> 00:18:54.385
And that's kind of what led to
the thinking that led to Flagship.

00:18:54.385 --> 00:18:56.260
KARIM LAKHANI: So tell
us what Flagship does.

00:18:56.260 --> 00:18:57.370
NOUBAR AFEYAN: Yeah.

00:18:57.370 --> 00:19:06.430
So Flagship is first and foremost
a life science innovation company.

00:19:06.430 --> 00:19:11.020
We have our own way by which we
innovate which I'll tell you about.

00:19:11.020 --> 00:19:15.940
Once we come up with something
that's worth taking forward,

00:19:15.940 --> 00:19:18.798
we form a company to take that forward.

00:19:18.798 --> 00:19:20.590
And so, we also had to
figure out how to do

00:19:20.590 --> 00:19:23.440
venture creation in a systematic way.

00:19:23.440 --> 00:19:26.680
We have our own capital that
we deploy into these companies

00:19:26.680 --> 00:19:29.530
to help them grow for the
first two, three years.

00:19:29.530 --> 00:19:33.370
So up until then, they operate as
wholly owned pieces of Flagship.

00:19:33.370 --> 00:19:34.810
And then, we spin them out.

00:19:34.810 --> 00:19:35.860
And we spin them out.

00:19:35.860 --> 00:19:38.950
And for the first time,
additional capital comes in.

00:19:38.950 --> 00:19:42.280
But by that time the culture,
the senior leadership

00:19:42.280 --> 00:19:47.050
team, the early proof of concept,
the early kind of customer or partner

00:19:47.050 --> 00:19:48.940
traction is there.

00:19:48.940 --> 00:19:52.430
And it looks like a company ready to
eventually live its life on its own.

00:19:52.430 --> 00:19:57.640
So we're not interested in building
kind of subsidiaries open endedly.

00:19:57.640 --> 00:20:01.450
Every single thing we do ends up
becoming a company or nothing.

00:20:01.450 --> 00:20:07.560
But so we've become kind of a creator of
companies around internal innovations.

00:20:07.560 --> 00:20:10.500
Now, sometimes these innovations
draw from external science.

00:20:10.500 --> 00:20:12.070
And I can describe that.

00:20:12.070 --> 00:20:15.767
But we're not only trying
to find science, and then

00:20:15.767 --> 00:20:17.850
find what to do with it,
and create the companies.

00:20:17.850 --> 00:20:20.350
That has become in vogue
the last few years.

00:20:20.350 --> 00:20:21.930
We've been doing this for 18 years.

00:20:21.930 --> 00:20:25.590
But we've ended up in a place
where we think the sustainable

00:20:25.590 --> 00:20:29.940
way to do this is to have your own
innovation capacity, your own company

00:20:29.940 --> 00:20:34.260
creation capacity, initial growth, and
then the companies have the wherewithal

00:20:34.260 --> 00:20:37.343
to grow and compete, and thrive.

00:20:37.343 --> 00:20:38.760
KARIM LAKHANI: That's fascinating.

00:20:38.760 --> 00:20:41.820
So walk us through one
example of a company

00:20:41.820 --> 00:20:44.400
that you guys have given
birth to from the start

00:20:44.400 --> 00:20:48.340
to its leaving the Flagship fold.

00:20:48.340 --> 00:20:49.590
NOUBAR AFEYAN: There are many.

00:20:49.590 --> 00:20:51.577
I think we've been
involved in doing about 50

00:20:51.577 --> 00:20:53.910
of these that are based on
internally developed science.

00:20:53.910 --> 00:20:56.520
And there's a whole bunch more
that also include external.

00:20:56.520 --> 00:21:00.030
But I'd say one of the internally
developed ones that just recently went

00:21:00.030 --> 00:21:02.100
public I can give as
an example is Moderna.

00:21:02.100 --> 00:21:03.860
So Moderna was--

00:21:03.860 --> 00:21:05.460
And I'll tell you kind of--

00:21:05.460 --> 00:21:12.847
we have this fairly let's say prescribed
process which the way people think

00:21:12.847 --> 00:21:15.930
about entrepreneurship and innovation,
especially breakthrough innovation,

00:21:15.930 --> 00:21:19.545
is that it is unruly, it is
chaotic, it is uninspired, and--

00:21:19.545 --> 00:21:20.670
KARIM LAKHANI: Serendipity.

00:21:20.670 --> 00:21:21.810
NOUBAR AFEYAN: It's
none of those things.

00:21:21.810 --> 00:21:22.650
KARIM LAKHANI: It's
none of those things.

00:21:22.650 --> 00:21:24.608
NOUBAR AFEYAN: Absolutely
none of those things.

00:21:24.608 --> 00:21:26.760
I have a completely
contrarian view of that.

00:21:26.760 --> 00:21:29.340
And I've done it enough times
in enough different fields

00:21:29.340 --> 00:21:32.650
that I think frankly, just
to be a little controversial,

00:21:32.650 --> 00:21:34.080
I think that's an illusion.

00:21:34.080 --> 00:21:39.450
In other words, I think that because
people haven't done that thing before,

00:21:39.450 --> 00:21:43.770
it's exciting and unexpected to them.

00:21:43.770 --> 00:21:47.610
But actually, pretty quickly it
looks like just another version

00:21:47.610 --> 00:21:50.860
of what came before them because
the historians come and say,

00:21:50.860 --> 00:21:53.760
well before there was the
iPad there was the iPod.

00:21:53.760 --> 00:21:56.220
And before that, there was
the Walkman, et cetera.

00:21:56.220 --> 00:21:59.460
And also, when you look at it that way,
you go, well why were you surprised?

00:21:59.460 --> 00:22:03.000
And that's when you realize that
these things are just evolving.

00:22:03.000 --> 00:22:06.870
And so, I'll give you an example
in this company called Moderna.

00:22:06.870 --> 00:22:10.080
But before that I'll tell you
that underlying how we think,

00:22:10.080 --> 00:22:12.450
you can do this
systematically, is the notion

00:22:12.450 --> 00:22:15.340
that breakthroughs
aren't made by people.

00:22:15.340 --> 00:22:16.290
They emerge.

00:22:16.290 --> 00:22:19.650
And the most understood,
not well understood,

00:22:19.650 --> 00:22:21.780
but understood version
of emergence that we

00:22:21.780 --> 00:22:27.240
know about is evolution, which is
that if you do variation, selection

00:22:27.240 --> 00:22:32.620
and iteration systematically,
the winners will emerge.

00:22:32.620 --> 00:22:35.850
And when they do, they
will look surprising.

00:22:35.850 --> 00:22:38.310
But actually they're the
result of that process.

00:22:38.310 --> 00:22:42.120
They're not some inspired thought that
has nothing to do with that process.

00:22:42.120 --> 00:22:46.240
So hold that thought as I give examples
that will all be examples of that.

00:22:46.240 --> 00:22:49.410
And that actually does cause
one to think differently

00:22:49.410 --> 00:22:53.160
about being possessive about all these
companies and all these innovations.

00:22:53.160 --> 00:22:54.420
We're not.

00:22:54.420 --> 00:22:56.760
We don't go around
saying, we invented this.

00:22:56.760 --> 00:22:59.100
We may file a patent
and get an invention.

00:22:59.100 --> 00:23:02.130
But where these ideas come
from are very emergent.

00:23:02.130 --> 00:23:04.875
So in Moderna's case,
talking about emergence,

00:23:04.875 --> 00:23:08.160
we understood by following the
various scientific advances

00:23:08.160 --> 00:23:12.780
that people were playing around with
converting fibroblast cells into what's

00:23:12.780 --> 00:23:15.190
called induced pluripotent stem cells.

00:23:15.190 --> 00:23:17.890
These are if somebody won a
Nobel Prize recently for this,

00:23:17.890 --> 00:23:19.330
these are called Yamanaka factors.

00:23:19.330 --> 00:23:22.350
So it turns out that if you
take differentiated cells

00:23:22.350 --> 00:23:25.680
and you introduce a couple of three
different proteins, four of them

00:23:25.680 --> 00:23:28.650
specifically, these cells
can de-differentiate

00:23:28.650 --> 00:23:30.210
and look like what came before them.

00:23:30.210 --> 00:23:30.990
Stem cells.

00:23:30.990 --> 00:23:35.040
They're not normal stem cells but
they're close to being stem cells.

00:23:35.040 --> 00:23:36.300
And so, why do that?

00:23:36.300 --> 00:23:38.230
Because then you could differentiate
it and make other cells.

00:23:38.230 --> 00:23:39.480
So that was the general field.

00:23:39.480 --> 00:23:43.650
And in that field, there have been some
people, particularly at UPenn and then

00:23:43.650 --> 00:23:46.020
more recently at Children's
Hospital here in Boston,

00:23:46.020 --> 00:23:48.150
who had been playing
around with using what's

00:23:48.150 --> 00:23:53.280
called mRNA, messenger RNA
to use these to go into cells

00:23:53.280 --> 00:23:54.690
and express these proteins.

00:23:54.690 --> 00:23:55.740
Why had they done that?

00:23:55.740 --> 00:23:58.260
Because it turns out this
mRNA, they could actually

00:23:58.260 --> 00:24:01.000
get a better transformation
of these cells.

00:24:01.000 --> 00:24:03.660
And so, we were aware of this work.

00:24:03.660 --> 00:24:06.370
And we started asking kind
of just what if questions.

00:24:06.370 --> 00:24:10.200
KARIM LAKHANI: So your team is
reading scientific papers, just

00:24:10.200 --> 00:24:11.520
in the air, yeah.

00:24:11.520 --> 00:24:11.700
NOUBAR AFEYAN: Yeah.

00:24:11.700 --> 00:24:12.220
Exactly.

00:24:12.220 --> 00:24:13.420
And we do this all the time.

00:24:13.420 --> 00:24:14.430
So and basically we're--

00:24:14.430 --> 00:24:16.590
KARIM LAKHANI: You're in the
midst of this flow of knowledge--

00:24:16.590 --> 00:24:16.770
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

00:24:16.770 --> 00:24:16.950
NOUBAR AFEYAN: Exactly.

00:24:16.950 --> 00:24:18.783
It's not about reading
papers for us as much

00:24:18.783 --> 00:24:21.360
as just, I mean we are
talking to lots of people

00:24:21.360 --> 00:24:22.690
about lots of different things.

00:24:22.690 --> 00:24:24.970
And then or somebody says, hey.

00:24:24.970 --> 00:24:27.450
By the way, I just saw this data.

00:24:27.450 --> 00:24:29.438
And then we actually
read papers in hindsight.

00:24:29.438 --> 00:24:31.230
In other words, we read
papers once we have

00:24:31.230 --> 00:24:33.780
an idea of what to do as
opposed to read papers

00:24:33.780 --> 00:24:38.280
to get an idea per se because the
signal-to-noise in papers is gigantic.

00:24:38.280 --> 00:24:39.260
You can't-- yeah.

00:24:39.260 --> 00:24:41.430
If you sit there and read
1,000 papers and that

00:24:41.430 --> 00:24:44.300
gives you three ideas,
hard to do anything else.

00:24:44.300 --> 00:24:48.030
So I'm sure that can be done too, but
that's not the way we approach it.

00:24:48.030 --> 00:24:52.170
Anyway, so long story short, we
started wondering for lots of reasons

00:24:52.170 --> 00:24:55.800
that I can't almost reproduce
because you'd have to go back to 2010

00:24:55.800 --> 00:24:59.170
in the summer and whatever else
was being thought of at the time.

00:24:59.170 --> 00:25:02.010
So understand by that time,
we had about 20 people

00:25:02.010 --> 00:25:07.490
that were doing this type of activity
in various combinations for a living.

00:25:07.490 --> 00:25:11.470
And so, it's important
because these ideas kind of

00:25:11.470 --> 00:25:14.350
get mixed and moved around.

00:25:14.350 --> 00:25:17.770
One person thinking deeply
is not going to get there.

00:25:17.770 --> 00:25:20.600
So we start asking,
can you think about--

00:25:20.600 --> 00:25:24.430
we started, why is there no
drug that is based on mRNA?

00:25:24.430 --> 00:25:27.550
Now, if you go back 10
years, drugs were made out

00:25:27.550 --> 00:25:29.260
of proteins and small molecules.

00:25:29.260 --> 00:25:33.100
There had been an effort to
use viruses to introduce genes

00:25:33.100 --> 00:25:34.750
into the body called gene therapy.

00:25:34.750 --> 00:25:39.730
And a person had died early on and
that whole field was shut down.

00:25:39.730 --> 00:25:43.330
And there really was no other variation
to proteins and small molecules.

00:25:43.330 --> 00:25:46.450
And there had been some efforts to
try to make, not to get too detailed,

00:25:46.450 --> 00:25:51.300
but other small DNA, RNA
molecules, antisense what's called,

00:25:51.300 --> 00:25:52.550
and we were aware of all this.

00:25:52.550 --> 00:25:55.738
But we're saying, why don't people
actually use this micro-RNA molecule?

00:25:55.738 --> 00:25:57.280
And that's just a thought experiment.

00:25:57.280 --> 00:25:59.585
And you can go and answer that question.

00:25:59.585 --> 00:26:01.210
KARIM LAKHANI: So you raised a problem.

00:26:01.210 --> 00:26:03.880
NOUBAR AFEYAN: You
raise a what if problem.

00:26:03.880 --> 00:26:07.900
Initially we don't start with,
we need to cure melanoma.

00:26:07.900 --> 00:26:12.045
We don't start a problem, what good
is this mRNA that can transform cells?

00:26:12.045 --> 00:26:14.170
Because it's not good for
anything other than that.

00:26:14.170 --> 00:26:16.480
But we just kind of
abstract it and said,

00:26:16.480 --> 00:26:20.290
is there a combination of
a solution and a problem

00:26:20.290 --> 00:26:24.970
that we can identify
simultaneously around this space?

00:26:24.970 --> 00:26:27.310
And if there isn't one, then we move on.

00:26:27.310 --> 00:26:30.130
And in this case, that's
part of our methodology.

00:26:30.130 --> 00:26:35.410
In this case, what we concluded was
that people had tried and failed

00:26:35.410 --> 00:26:36.503
10 years earlier.

00:26:36.503 --> 00:26:38.170
We understood why they tried and failed.

00:26:38.170 --> 00:26:40.550
We realized that there
was no obvious fix to it.

00:26:40.550 --> 00:26:43.870
But there was a direction of fixing
that you could modify chemically

00:26:43.870 --> 00:26:45.430
some of these RNA molecules.

00:26:45.430 --> 00:26:48.288
And they at least hinted
that if you kept doing that,

00:26:48.288 --> 00:26:49.330
there may be better ways.

00:26:49.330 --> 00:26:50.260
We had no proof.

00:26:50.260 --> 00:26:54.010
There was no academic literature
that said this can be done.

00:26:54.010 --> 00:26:56.680
Now we could have waited
for that but we probably

00:26:56.680 --> 00:26:58.900
would have been five, six
years because now there

00:26:58.900 --> 00:27:01.750
is academic papers from which
we could have started companies.

00:27:01.750 --> 00:27:08.200
But our whole thing is to almost
mine this science of the future

00:27:08.200 --> 00:27:12.460
to try to solve problems of today
versus mine the science of today

00:27:12.460 --> 00:27:14.210
to solve problems of the future.

00:27:14.210 --> 00:27:17.640
And that is exactly backwards.

00:27:17.640 --> 00:27:21.140
And it's not any better, it's just
that you look at the world differently.

00:27:21.140 --> 00:27:25.610
So publications give you
evidence for what you want to do.

00:27:25.610 --> 00:27:27.710
But that's not why they
publish those things.

00:27:27.710 --> 00:27:29.170
They were thinking of
a very different thing.

00:27:29.170 --> 00:27:30.337
Do you know what I'm saying?

00:27:30.337 --> 00:27:32.590
So in that regard, that's a little bit.

00:27:32.590 --> 00:27:35.768
So in Moderna's case, we identified--

00:27:35.768 --> 00:27:38.560
well the other thing that we do,
a very integral part of what we do

00:27:38.560 --> 00:27:40.210
is that we don't do this in a vacuum.

00:27:40.210 --> 00:27:44.215
So we go out and talk to
people about our hypotheses.

00:27:44.215 --> 00:27:44.840
These are not--

00:27:44.840 --> 00:27:46.690
KARIM LAKHANI: And you're not
afraid of people taking your ideas?

00:27:46.690 --> 00:27:47.080
NOUBAR AFEYAN: No.

00:27:47.080 --> 00:27:48.100
And I'll tell you why.

00:27:51.070 --> 00:27:52.950
In order to try to
create an evolution-- so

00:27:52.950 --> 00:27:55.075
we're trying to create an
evolutionary environment.

00:27:55.075 --> 00:27:56.620
How do you do that?

00:27:56.620 --> 00:28:00.195
The scientific method uses
hypotheses and experimentation.

00:28:00.195 --> 00:28:01.570
So we actually do the same thing.

00:28:01.570 --> 00:28:03.910
We create value hypotheses.

00:28:03.910 --> 00:28:09.880
We basically say, I will take an mRNA
molecule formulated with this agent,

00:28:09.880 --> 00:28:13.027
stick it into muscle, use
muscle to-- none of it is true.

00:28:13.027 --> 00:28:14.360
None of it has been done before.

00:28:14.360 --> 00:28:15.910
But it's just a starting point.

00:28:15.910 --> 00:28:20.680
We then go with that to a handful
of folks that are both experts

00:28:20.680 --> 00:28:22.358
and non-experts in the field.

00:28:22.358 --> 00:28:25.150
Super important thing if you're
going to practice this kind of work

00:28:25.150 --> 00:28:27.940
is that experts actually
are not the people who

00:28:27.940 --> 00:28:30.970
are going to give you an idea
of how to do things dramatically

00:28:30.970 --> 00:28:35.140
differently because they became experts
by already doing something dramatically

00:28:35.140 --> 00:28:38.000
differently and now they're
defending their territory.

00:28:38.000 --> 00:28:42.320
So if you go to talk to somebody
and say, can this be done out here?

00:28:42.320 --> 00:28:44.740
Most people we talked
to, they can tell you

00:28:44.740 --> 00:28:48.280
precisely why it can't be done, which
is really valuable from a selection

00:28:48.280 --> 00:28:51.160
pressure standpoint, but
not the source of kind of,

00:28:51.160 --> 00:28:53.710
and here's a completely different way.

00:28:53.710 --> 00:28:56.890
So other people though
who don't have expertise

00:28:56.890 --> 00:28:59.230
but have interesting creativity--

00:28:59.230 --> 00:29:01.373
So what we do is we take our hypotheses.

00:29:01.373 --> 00:29:03.790
And it's never one because one
of the things we've learned

00:29:03.790 --> 00:29:06.350
is that if you start
with one starting point,

00:29:06.350 --> 00:29:09.530
you only locally search
the space around it.

00:29:09.530 --> 00:29:11.680
So we sample multiple different points.

00:29:11.680 --> 00:29:17.780
So it might be, how about mRNA as a way
to make more proteins in a fermenter?

00:29:17.780 --> 00:29:18.280
How about--

00:29:18.280 --> 00:29:21.190
So around the theme, we come up
with totally different valuable

00:29:21.190 --> 00:29:22.420
we try to identify.

00:29:22.420 --> 00:29:25.750
And then, we put a starting
point and then we start talking.

00:29:25.750 --> 00:29:28.960
And what has been interesting and a
great learning exercise over the years

00:29:28.960 --> 00:29:35.020
that we've seen is that if you
go to somebody with a bad idea

00:29:35.020 --> 00:29:39.445
but you actually present
it in a precise way,

00:29:39.445 --> 00:29:41.390
they will tell you why it's a bad idea.

00:29:41.390 --> 00:29:44.350
And in that transaction,
something happens.

00:29:44.350 --> 00:29:47.890
They feel smarter and you
get selection pressure.

00:29:47.890 --> 00:29:50.560
And so, the way our process
works is that we actually

00:29:50.560 --> 00:29:52.520
do this with multiple people.

00:29:52.520 --> 00:29:56.170
So when you said, aren't you afraid
they're going to take your idea?

00:29:56.170 --> 00:29:57.460
What are they taking?

00:29:57.460 --> 00:29:58.690
We just made it up.

00:29:58.690 --> 00:30:00.310
There's zero fact to it.

00:30:00.310 --> 00:30:01.550
Literally zero fact.

00:30:01.550 --> 00:30:02.050
So--

00:30:02.050 --> 00:30:03.000
KARIM LAKHANI: Just
imagining a hypothesis.

00:30:03.000 --> 00:30:03.420
NOUBAR AFEYAN: Exactly.

00:30:03.420 --> 00:30:04.580
We're just a hypothesis.

00:30:04.580 --> 00:30:05.288
And they know it.

00:30:05.288 --> 00:30:07.372
Because then they tell us,
what proof do you have?

00:30:07.372 --> 00:30:07.940
We say, none.

00:30:07.940 --> 00:30:10.400
So then they still engage
because it's a almost--

00:30:10.400 --> 00:30:13.160
when we talk to these folks, and
this is what we're known for,

00:30:13.160 --> 00:30:17.640
we create a bit of an expert
free zone in the sense that you--

00:30:17.640 --> 00:30:18.140
See, look.

00:30:18.140 --> 00:30:21.590
These scientists, here we are
at Harvard, and MIT, Stanford,

00:30:21.590 --> 00:30:26.510
these people go to the meetings of their
fields and they cannot say something

00:30:26.510 --> 00:30:30.360
that does not seem unbelievably
smart and reasonable.

00:30:30.360 --> 00:30:32.990
Actually I'll make the
point, let me caveat.

00:30:32.990 --> 00:30:37.520
Reasonableness in my view
is the gravitational force

00:30:37.520 --> 00:30:39.260
that keeps innovation from happening.

00:30:39.260 --> 00:30:43.370
Because people enforce reasonableness
in the scientific world

00:30:43.370 --> 00:30:45.440
on what you are allowed to say.

00:30:45.440 --> 00:30:47.510
You go publish something
in the literature that's

00:30:47.510 --> 00:30:49.950
not reasonable to the
reviewers, you can't publish it.

00:30:49.950 --> 00:30:51.200
KARIM LAKHANI: You get killed.

00:30:51.200 --> 00:30:52.280
I've done a whole study on that.

00:30:52.280 --> 00:30:52.540
Right.

00:30:52.540 --> 00:30:54.500
NOUBAR AFEYAN: So ask
yourself the question,

00:30:54.500 --> 00:30:58.040
why is it that we think that the
only innovations worth working on

00:30:58.040 --> 00:30:59.960
start their life reasonably?

00:30:59.960 --> 00:31:03.650
In fact, I can show you many
innovations that are now

00:31:03.650 --> 00:31:05.760
accepted as real transformative things.

00:31:05.760 --> 00:31:08.480
Every one of them started
life unreasonably.

00:31:08.480 --> 00:31:12.890
And yet, we go look for
them in reasonable places

00:31:12.890 --> 00:31:17.640
by reasonable people who are using
reasonableness filters to kill them.

00:31:17.640 --> 00:31:20.810
So because we knew that, and this
was not the first thing I had done,

00:31:20.810 --> 00:31:22.012
we kind of say, look.

00:31:22.012 --> 00:31:23.720
Let's forget reasonableness
for a second.

00:31:23.720 --> 00:31:24.560
Just think with us.

00:31:24.560 --> 00:31:26.210
And they tell us, bad idea.

00:31:26.210 --> 00:31:27.650
Somebody in Japan had tried it.

00:31:27.650 --> 00:31:28.820
The experiment won't work.

00:31:28.820 --> 00:31:30.920
And we keep tweaking and
tweaking and tweaking.

00:31:30.920 --> 00:31:34.580
This is different than kind of like
you hear a lot in Silicon Valley

00:31:34.580 --> 00:31:36.410
speak kind of a pivoting.

00:31:36.410 --> 00:31:37.340
This is not pivoting.

00:31:37.340 --> 00:31:39.290
Evolution is the master pivot.

00:31:39.290 --> 00:31:41.120
It's nonstop pivoting.

00:31:41.120 --> 00:31:42.680
There's nothing to pivot from and to.

00:31:42.680 --> 00:31:44.480
You're just adapting and surviving.

00:31:44.480 --> 00:31:47.630
So that mindset is super important.

00:31:47.630 --> 00:31:51.110
Now, what happens is over time,
things emerge or they don't.

00:31:51.110 --> 00:31:55.070
But they emerge where the
next person you talk to goes,

00:31:55.070 --> 00:31:56.660
they can't find fault with it.

00:31:56.660 --> 00:31:58.500
You know that because
it frustrates them.

00:31:58.500 --> 00:32:00.890
And then, they start telling
you, well do you have any proof?

00:32:00.890 --> 00:32:01.610
And you go, no, no, no.

00:32:01.610 --> 00:32:02.370
I just made this up.

00:32:02.370 --> 00:32:03.912
It's just that we've been [INAUDIBLE]

00:32:03.912 --> 00:32:07.220
And then, that point is when we
go into a lab for the first time.

00:32:07.220 --> 00:32:09.770
And then and only then
do we test the idea.

00:32:09.770 --> 00:32:11.840
Now, you can't test it fully.

00:32:11.840 --> 00:32:15.150
You can't test it to where
it takes years and years.

00:32:15.150 --> 00:32:17.895
But what we try to--
and one of the expertise

00:32:17.895 --> 00:32:22.760
we've developed over the years is
how to figure out what to test first.

00:32:22.760 --> 00:32:27.080
And this in life science, you can't
use these words killer experiment

00:32:27.080 --> 00:32:29.480
because life science, it can kill.

00:32:29.480 --> 00:32:32.330
But you want to do the
killer experiment early.

00:32:32.330 --> 00:32:36.290
And so, figuring out what's the killer
experiment or two, or three, or four,

00:32:36.290 --> 00:32:40.970
that if it doesn't fail allows you
to go forward, that's the trick.

00:32:40.970 --> 00:32:42.770
And we do that in our shop.

00:32:42.770 --> 00:32:45.380
So in Moderna's case, we
spent a couple million dollars

00:32:45.380 --> 00:32:48.730
about six months doing
a set of key things

00:32:48.730 --> 00:32:50.480
but for which we would
not have proceeded.

00:32:50.480 --> 00:32:52.940
So we knew the idea
had morphed and evolved

00:32:52.940 --> 00:32:55.190
and it had some resilience to it.

00:32:55.190 --> 00:32:57.530
We went into labs and
asked specific questions.

00:32:57.530 --> 00:33:00.410
Once we saw that actually some of
the things we were hypothesizing

00:33:00.410 --> 00:33:03.680
could be done partially,
we then decided, OK.

00:33:03.680 --> 00:33:08.450
Let's form the best economic value
creation engine known to mankind

00:33:08.450 --> 00:33:10.160
which is a startup in my view.

00:33:10.160 --> 00:33:11.090
I'm biased.

00:33:11.090 --> 00:33:14.690
And then, we deploy that startup
to go after that value pool

00:33:14.690 --> 00:33:16.400
in as many different ways as we can.

00:33:16.400 --> 00:33:19.320
So that's kind of both
Moderna and the process.

00:33:19.320 --> 00:33:24.110
So in 2010 summer, we did
what we call exploration.

00:33:24.110 --> 00:33:25.580
We tend to start these things.

00:33:25.580 --> 00:33:27.740
We have our own kind of terminology.

00:33:27.740 --> 00:33:28.970
We don't start companies.

00:33:28.970 --> 00:33:31.880
One of the things I had
learned throughout the '90s

00:33:31.880 --> 00:33:35.090
is that companies are
kind of almost immortal.

00:33:35.090 --> 00:33:37.260
Once you start them
it's hard to kill them.

00:33:37.260 --> 00:33:40.368
So we thought instead of fighting
that, just don't start a company.

00:33:40.368 --> 00:33:42.410
So we start these things
we call proto-companies.

00:33:42.410 --> 00:33:43.640
They're prototypes.

00:33:43.640 --> 00:33:44.960
And we don't name them.

00:33:44.960 --> 00:33:45.950
We number them.

00:33:45.950 --> 00:33:47.330
We don't give people titles.

00:33:47.330 --> 00:33:49.760
And it's like a project
except it's a proto-company.

00:33:49.760 --> 00:33:53.150
It wants to be-- just like a prototype
product, it wants to be a product.

00:33:53.150 --> 00:33:56.030
But you can beat it up, you can
kill it, you can loan it, whatever.

00:33:56.030 --> 00:33:56.900
That's how we work.

00:33:56.900 --> 00:33:58.260
And we've numbered them.

00:33:58.260 --> 00:33:58.910
We're now at, I don't know.

00:33:58.910 --> 00:34:00.952
We've probably done about
100 of these over time.

00:34:00.952 --> 00:34:06.180
But I think we're on number 65 because
we reset these every few years.

00:34:06.180 --> 00:34:11.210
And so, Moderna became I think it
was 24 was the number it had, or 18.

00:34:11.210 --> 00:34:12.420
I forget which one it was.

00:34:12.420 --> 00:34:15.110
And then in 2011 we went into labs.

00:34:15.110 --> 00:34:19.595
And about six months later, we started
realizing the data was functional.

00:34:19.595 --> 00:34:24.170
We brought in, at the time, at the
early stage of the company a CEO.

00:34:24.170 --> 00:34:26.239
Usually we get a CEO much later.

00:34:26.239 --> 00:34:28.550
Happened to be that I
knew someone who had

00:34:28.550 --> 00:34:31.340
kind of really interesting
experience not in doing this,

00:34:31.340 --> 00:34:34.699
because a lot of our CEOs
have not done this before.

00:34:34.699 --> 00:34:39.350
So we experiment with people who
have kind of more of the personality.

00:34:39.350 --> 00:34:41.179
And we can talk about it if you like.

00:34:41.179 --> 00:34:46.552
But in 2012 on, the company has
grown as a standalone company

00:34:46.552 --> 00:34:47.719
and reached certain heights.

00:34:47.719 --> 00:34:51.710
And now, today it has 21 clinical
programs of different drugs

00:34:51.710 --> 00:34:54.770
that it's trying to develop, about
12 that have gone into humans.

00:34:54.770 --> 00:34:56.845
And it's about 700 person company.

00:34:56.845 --> 00:34:58.970
KARIM LAKHANI: And you had
just had an IPO as well.

00:34:58.970 --> 00:35:00.020
NOUBAR AFEYAN: We just did an IPO.

00:35:00.020 --> 00:35:00.290
Yes.

00:35:00.290 --> 00:35:02.082
It has been private
work for quite a while.

00:35:02.082 --> 00:35:02.830
But yeah.

00:35:02.830 --> 00:35:06.940
And this is what I just described to you
is the similar case in a company called

00:35:06.940 --> 00:35:11.080
Rubius that figured out how to take red
blood cells and make therapeutics out

00:35:11.080 --> 00:35:14.398
of them, which nobody academically
had proposed as a therapeutic.

00:35:14.398 --> 00:35:16.690
People were playing with red
blood cells, people were--

00:35:16.690 --> 00:35:19.540
Again, one of the things about it
is that academic science for us

00:35:19.540 --> 00:35:24.160
is an incredibly important
substrate that we recompose in order

00:35:24.160 --> 00:35:27.325
to do this type of science versus--

00:35:27.325 --> 00:35:30.760
so I'll tell you, the stark contrast,
again they both work, but is

00:35:30.760 --> 00:35:36.010
that the way science is usually done is
money goes in to expand the boundaries.

00:35:36.010 --> 00:35:39.040
Depending on how much money
anyone's place in the boundary

00:35:39.040 --> 00:35:43.150
gets, how hard that terrain is,
and how insistent the person is,

00:35:43.150 --> 00:35:45.280
that defines how quickly
that piece expands.

00:35:45.280 --> 00:35:49.720
And over time as this expands, some
value opportunities come along.

00:35:49.720 --> 00:35:52.990
And then, entrepreneurs
otherwise known as opportunists

00:35:52.990 --> 00:35:56.140
in a better sense of the word,
positive sense of the word that I use,

00:35:56.140 --> 00:35:58.000
find places where value can be created.

00:35:58.000 --> 00:35:59.170
And they start companies.

00:35:59.170 --> 00:36:03.900
So there's this adjacency
oriented value creation

00:36:03.900 --> 00:36:08.800
that is science, leading to innovation,
leading to products and value.

00:36:08.800 --> 00:36:13.090
In our case, we start with
the kind of or let's say,

00:36:13.090 --> 00:36:14.800
foreseeing potential value.

00:36:14.800 --> 00:36:16.720
Totally hypothetical.

00:36:16.720 --> 00:36:19.780
Finding the appropriate
science from which

00:36:19.780 --> 00:36:22.600
we could somehow link to that product.

00:36:22.600 --> 00:36:25.640
And then, figuring out whether
you can get there from here.

00:36:25.640 --> 00:36:29.440
And that allows us to
try things that look

00:36:29.440 --> 00:36:32.080
as an illusion completely surprising.

00:36:32.080 --> 00:36:33.262
But actually follows--

00:36:33.262 --> 00:36:34.720
KARIM LAKHANI: [INAUDIBLE] process.

00:36:34.720 --> 00:36:38.110
Can you give us an example
of-- so you describe Moderna

00:36:38.110 --> 00:36:40.390
as an internally developed idea.

00:36:40.390 --> 00:36:43.160
You've done a few that
were externally sourced.

00:36:43.160 --> 00:36:46.835
Can you walk us through a journey like
that where you saw something and--

00:36:46.835 --> 00:36:47.710
NOUBAR AFEYAN: Right.

00:36:47.710 --> 00:36:50.360
And so, in that case, I mean
there's several I can give you.

00:36:50.360 --> 00:36:54.160
But a very recent one
that we've been working on

00:36:54.160 --> 00:36:57.010
is a company that's today
called foghorn but started

00:36:57.010 --> 00:37:04.750
its life as a academic project where
Gerry Crabtree and Cigall Kadoch who

00:37:04.750 --> 00:37:08.980
are experts in the field of kind of the
biochemistry of chromatin remodeling

00:37:08.980 --> 00:37:12.160
and the protein complex that
it takes to actually that

00:37:12.160 --> 00:37:15.940
governs aspects of regulating
expression of genes.

00:37:15.940 --> 00:37:19.390
They had made some findings
around that complex, very, very

00:37:19.390 --> 00:37:23.020
precise biochemistry
and molecular biology,

00:37:23.020 --> 00:37:25.600
that had led to some
interesting findings of things

00:37:25.600 --> 00:37:27.550
that could be drugged in targets.

00:37:27.550 --> 00:37:30.650
Early in the field, a
lot more work to do.

00:37:30.650 --> 00:37:33.640
But clearly we could
associate with some diseases.

00:37:33.640 --> 00:37:36.490
By the time one of these
things gets to a place

00:37:36.490 --> 00:37:40.090
where there's already a whole kind
of infrastructure put around it,

00:37:40.090 --> 00:37:41.860
then you really just need capital.

00:37:41.860 --> 00:37:45.010
I mean, you need capital with
experience but you need capital.

00:37:45.010 --> 00:37:49.900
In this case, what to do, and how to
do it, and in what sequence to do it,

00:37:49.900 --> 00:37:52.630
and who to do it with, all of
that is an open ended thing.

00:37:52.630 --> 00:37:56.020
It's really more suggestive science.

00:37:56.020 --> 00:37:58.670
The closest we get to doing
things externally is that.

00:37:58.670 --> 00:38:01.300
And in this case, we worked
with them to come up first

00:38:01.300 --> 00:38:03.280
without licensing the
technology or anything.

00:38:03.280 --> 00:38:05.650
We first worked on, is
this even worth something?

00:38:05.650 --> 00:38:07.930
So we did an exploration
that was inspired

00:38:07.930 --> 00:38:12.130
by that starting point versus a
completely hypothetical starting point.

00:38:12.130 --> 00:38:13.720
Still very poorly defined.

00:38:13.720 --> 00:38:16.840
Still followed coming up
with some value hypotheses,

00:38:16.840 --> 00:38:19.750
engaging different
discussions, iterating around,

00:38:19.750 --> 00:38:22.240
what's the version of
this worth creating?

00:38:22.240 --> 00:38:25.580
And then, we formed a proto-company,
tested, did some more experiments.

00:38:25.580 --> 00:38:28.330
Now in this case, they were
experiments we could piggyback off of.

00:38:28.330 --> 00:38:32.500
But they were as you know fully, half
of academic experiments cannot be

00:38:32.500 --> 00:38:34.800
reproduced and do not
when they're taken.

00:38:34.800 --> 00:38:35.800
Nothing wrong with that.

00:38:35.800 --> 00:38:37.220
But we have to test it.

00:38:37.220 --> 00:38:40.780
And so, we did that and we could see
that this was a really fertile space.

00:38:40.780 --> 00:38:42.130
We attracted a team.

00:38:42.130 --> 00:38:44.012
Some of our team went in the beginning.

00:38:44.012 --> 00:38:45.220
And then we attracted a team.

00:38:45.220 --> 00:38:48.040
And now, that company is emerging.

00:38:48.040 --> 00:38:53.740
It's probably a year plus away from
having a clinical candidate to test.

00:38:53.740 --> 00:38:58.462
But it's becoming known as a real
pioneer in that field of biology.

00:38:58.462 --> 00:39:01.420
KARIM LAKHANI: So in this process
you describe as evolutionary process,

00:39:01.420 --> 00:39:05.890
both for Foghorn and for
Moderna, and the general process,

00:39:05.890 --> 00:39:09.410
you're sort of out there
doing this hypothesis testing.

00:39:09.410 --> 00:39:14.350
How do you step away
from confirmation bias?

00:39:14.350 --> 00:39:18.212
Which is-- how do you stop
yourselves from not fooling--

00:39:18.212 --> 00:39:19.420
NOUBAR AFEYAN: A lot of ways.

00:39:19.420 --> 00:39:20.140
A lot of ways.

00:39:20.140 --> 00:39:21.760
A very good point.

00:39:21.760 --> 00:39:24.490
So first of all, scale.

00:39:24.490 --> 00:39:27.650
We do 100 explorations a year.

00:39:27.650 --> 00:39:28.820
So why is that important?

00:39:28.820 --> 00:39:32.140
Because if we did two,
we'd have a lot of bias.

00:39:32.140 --> 00:39:35.800
But we are drowning
in things we could do.

00:39:35.800 --> 00:39:39.950
Again, and if we open the aperture to
include external things, it'd be 1,000.

00:39:39.950 --> 00:39:43.760
So that's not the rate limiting
step, what things to work on or not.

00:39:43.760 --> 00:39:48.610
Second, we've carefully
designed both the team we have

00:39:48.610 --> 00:39:52.900
and the way we reward the players
such that they're constantly

00:39:52.900 --> 00:39:56.560
trying to figure out how quickly they
could kill an idea so they could work

00:39:56.560 --> 00:39:59.860
on a better one which is
the exact opposite of what

00:39:59.860 --> 00:40:03.520
happens in solo entrepreneurship
where if you kill the idea,

00:40:03.520 --> 00:40:04.418
you're unemployed.

00:40:04.418 --> 00:40:07.460
And while you might have another idea
to work on, you start from scratch.

00:40:07.460 --> 00:40:12.280
So in this proto-co phase, we have many
more proto-cos than we could prosecute.

00:40:12.280 --> 00:40:15.130
So out of the 100, maybe 10 or 15.

00:40:15.130 --> 00:40:19.030
Not the best 10 or 15 by the way,
an absolutely arbitrary 10 or 15.

00:40:19.030 --> 00:40:22.480
I mean it's best based on the
fit between the people who

00:40:22.480 --> 00:40:27.940
are working on it and but we don't
have any kind of filtering process.

00:40:27.940 --> 00:40:29.800
We do these explorations.

00:40:29.800 --> 00:40:35.230
We have five distinct teams, each about
10 people, all PhDs, MDs, all of whom

00:40:35.230 --> 00:40:39.550
have grown up within our infrastructure,
that in teams of two or three

00:40:39.550 --> 00:40:42.370
do these expirations,
propose proto-companies.

00:40:42.370 --> 00:40:44.800
Then a handful of them get
taken into proto-company phase.

00:40:44.800 --> 00:40:46.810
Each one gets a million to $2 million.

00:40:46.810 --> 00:40:50.920
And then, they run the
gauntlet of proving it wrong.

00:40:50.920 --> 00:40:53.500
One way you get no confirmation
bias is that everybody

00:40:53.500 --> 00:40:55.750
knows that if they're
working on this, they're

00:40:55.750 --> 00:40:59.170
not working on the next Moderna
or the next Rubius, or--

00:40:59.170 --> 00:41:01.420
So it's not perfect.

00:41:01.420 --> 00:41:04.870
And of course, there's the third
element is that people like me

00:41:04.870 --> 00:41:07.430
and many others now who
have done this for 15,

00:41:07.430 --> 00:41:10.660
20 years in the Flagship
context, we're 18 years old now,

00:41:10.660 --> 00:41:14.950
are there to kind of keep
reminding people that they

00:41:14.950 --> 00:41:16.240
should question themselves.

00:41:16.240 --> 00:41:21.340
I mean, one of the things I
kind of deduced, everybody

00:41:21.340 --> 00:41:24.790
who does this long enough comes
up with their own kind of words

00:41:24.790 --> 00:41:25.880
to say similar things.

00:41:25.880 --> 00:41:30.250
In my case, the mindset that I think
saves you from this kind of bias

00:41:30.250 --> 00:41:33.580
as much as possible is that
of a paranoid optimist.

00:41:33.580 --> 00:41:36.820
And so, I for a long time
tried to get across this notion

00:41:36.820 --> 00:41:40.120
that if you're going
to be in this space,

00:41:40.120 --> 00:41:43.090
if you're a paranoid optimist--
by that I mean you're not

00:41:43.090 --> 00:41:45.910
so optimistic that you
actually get reckless,

00:41:45.910 --> 00:41:48.700
but you're not so paranoid
that you get depressed.

00:41:48.700 --> 00:41:53.290
And this constantly questioning
whether it really will work,

00:41:53.290 --> 00:41:55.360
or how big it will be,
or whether you should

00:41:55.360 --> 00:41:59.380
be doing something else,
that doubt, doubtful optimism

00:41:59.380 --> 00:42:02.950
which is really a tortured state
to be in, is perfect for evolution.

00:42:02.950 --> 00:42:04.113
It makes you poised.

00:42:04.113 --> 00:42:06.280
It makes you constantly
say, but can I do it better?

00:42:06.280 --> 00:42:07.420
Can I do differently?

00:42:07.420 --> 00:42:08.630
Who else is doing it?

00:42:08.630 --> 00:42:12.850
And so, that is a fourth way maybe
of protecting against the bias,

00:42:12.850 --> 00:42:16.210
is that we could not
believe in what we're

00:42:16.210 --> 00:42:20.500
saying long enough because
the doubt constantly kicks in.

00:42:20.500 --> 00:42:22.810
And then, we don't stay
doubtful long enough

00:42:22.810 --> 00:42:24.470
that we don't actually take some risk.

00:42:24.470 --> 00:42:27.283
So it's really it's like a
gas pedal and a brake pedal.

00:42:27.283 --> 00:42:29.200
You don't want to press
them at the same time.

00:42:29.200 --> 00:42:32.200
But if you toggle between them and
you've got a steering wheel, at least

00:42:32.200 --> 00:42:34.360
simplistically I think
you can go places.

00:42:34.360 --> 00:42:36.910
KARIM LAKHANI: For the
people taking our course,

00:42:36.910 --> 00:42:39.230
they won't have access to a Flagship.

00:42:39.230 --> 00:42:40.750
They'll be around the world.

00:42:40.750 --> 00:42:42.670
They'll have access to some technology.

00:42:42.670 --> 00:42:46.570
And they'll have to sort of
develop these value hypotheses.

00:42:46.570 --> 00:42:48.396
What advice would you give them?

00:42:48.396 --> 00:42:52.420
So they're come across
something from our course,

00:42:52.420 --> 00:42:55.360
or something in their own
companies, or in the labs,

00:42:55.360 --> 00:42:56.860
or the universities near them.

00:42:56.860 --> 00:43:00.040
And what advice would you
give them in terms of how

00:43:00.040 --> 00:43:01.863
to follow your process on their own?

00:43:01.863 --> 00:43:02.905
NOUBAR AFEYAN: Of course.

00:43:02.905 --> 00:43:03.280
Of course.

00:43:03.280 --> 00:43:03.760
Of course.

00:43:03.760 --> 00:43:05.177
No, and in fact, I think they can.

00:43:05.177 --> 00:43:09.130
I think some of what we do has
to do with the institution.

00:43:09.130 --> 00:43:13.150
But a lot of what we do is
a mindset and a methodology.

00:43:13.150 --> 00:43:15.170
And that is not dependent
on the institution.

00:43:15.170 --> 00:43:19.720
So I'd say first and foremost
again, everything I'm saying

00:43:19.720 --> 00:43:22.810
has to do with science based
companies because there's

00:43:22.810 --> 00:43:26.920
a lot of other companies that are market
based and product innovation based

00:43:26.920 --> 00:43:30.250
and execution based that a lot of
this stuff is maybe overkill for.

00:43:30.250 --> 00:43:30.295
KARIM LAKHANI: Yeah.

00:43:30.295 --> 00:43:32.370
I don't think there will
be any apps in our--

00:43:32.370 --> 00:43:36.170
So a science based company,
I'd say a few things.

00:43:36.170 --> 00:43:39.100
One, you've got to ask
yourself whether you really

00:43:39.100 --> 00:43:45.610
believe that the solution should emerge
versus the solution should be asserted

00:43:45.610 --> 00:43:49.120
and that you just have to
go look for an application.

00:43:49.120 --> 00:43:53.020
I believe that if you
really think rigorously,

00:43:53.020 --> 00:43:58.540
you may have a technology for which you
try to find a problem worth solving.

00:43:58.540 --> 00:44:00.310
But then that problem
may be better solved

00:44:00.310 --> 00:44:02.290
with something else
which you might also be

00:44:02.290 --> 00:44:04.900
able to go get which might
lead you to another problem.

00:44:04.900 --> 00:44:08.650
And those iteration loops might get
you a lot more of a valuable place

00:44:08.650 --> 00:44:11.020
than just saying, I'm a matchmaker.

00:44:11.020 --> 00:44:12.130
This technology.

00:44:12.130 --> 00:44:13.030
This application.

00:44:13.030 --> 00:44:13.760
I'm done.

00:44:13.760 --> 00:44:16.990
So first is if you really want
to dedicate the next five, 10

00:44:16.990 --> 00:44:20.110
years of your life, which in
life science is a short frame.

00:44:20.110 --> 00:44:22.060
And in the tech world, it's two years.

00:44:22.060 --> 00:44:23.980
It works, doesn't work, you're out.

00:44:23.980 --> 00:44:27.160
So that's one thing is I'd say
think about your own version

00:44:27.160 --> 00:44:28.870
of an emergent process.

00:44:28.870 --> 00:44:32.470
And don't prematurely stop
variation, stop selection.

00:44:32.470 --> 00:44:35.680
And by the way, don't also just
outsource selection to investors

00:44:35.680 --> 00:44:38.290
because if you're an
entrepreneur and you say,

00:44:38.290 --> 00:44:39.858
I will come up with the solution.

00:44:39.858 --> 00:44:41.650
And then, let's see
what they'll invest in.

00:44:41.650 --> 00:44:44.950
You'll be optimizing
for investability, not

00:44:44.950 --> 00:44:49.840
for what's worth building, what's going
to make the biggest reward for you,

00:44:49.840 --> 00:44:51.580
society, what's impactful.

00:44:51.580 --> 00:44:57.440
It'll purely be custom made
to those days' investors.

00:44:57.440 --> 00:44:59.440
And by the way, the reason
people get frustrated

00:44:59.440 --> 00:45:01.773
is that every investor is
looking for a different thing.

00:45:01.773 --> 00:45:03.125
And so, they think it's noise.

00:45:03.125 --> 00:45:05.510
And they can't get selection
pressure out of it.

00:45:05.510 --> 00:45:06.710
I think that's a good thing.

00:45:06.710 --> 00:45:11.690
They should themselves get
selection pressure from customers,

00:45:11.690 --> 00:45:16.670
from people who are going to actually
prescribe the medicine, or patients,

00:45:16.670 --> 00:45:19.160
or a combination of things.

00:45:19.160 --> 00:45:20.960
So that's one set of things.

00:45:20.960 --> 00:45:26.185
I think the second thing that
this mindset gives you is,

00:45:26.185 --> 00:45:27.560
and we haven't talked about this.

00:45:27.560 --> 00:45:29.560
So let me just bring that
out, is that every one

00:45:29.560 --> 00:45:32.707
of these kind of company
ideas, big breakthroughs,

00:45:32.707 --> 00:45:34.040
could be technology out of labs.

00:45:34.040 --> 00:45:36.930
And by the way, I think it's a
perfectly good starting point.

00:45:36.930 --> 00:45:41.090
You want to think about it in its
ultimate embodiment as possibly

00:45:41.090 --> 00:45:44.360
many, many different companies.

00:45:44.360 --> 00:45:47.840
So a seed that can give rise to--

00:45:47.840 --> 00:45:50.480
I mean think of almost a fertilized egg.

00:45:50.480 --> 00:45:54.350
That is an omnipotent cell when
it comes to differentiation.

00:45:54.350 --> 00:45:57.260
It could make liver cells,
it could make cardiac cells,

00:45:57.260 --> 00:46:00.690
it could make immune cells,
it can go many different ways.

00:46:00.690 --> 00:46:04.220
So if you think about all those ways,
if you think about all those ways,

00:46:04.220 --> 00:46:08.270
then you have to ask yourself,
how long can I keep optionality

00:46:08.270 --> 00:46:09.620
even while I'm building value?

00:46:09.620 --> 00:46:11.210
Because if you just sit
there and go, oh my God.

00:46:11.210 --> 00:46:13.100
I could be anything,
you don't do anything.

00:46:13.100 --> 00:46:19.100
But the question becomes, if I have
direction A that locks me into becoming

00:46:19.100 --> 00:46:21.380
a skin cell and that's it, I'm done.

00:46:21.380 --> 00:46:25.490
Or a cell that can still
maintain optionality.

00:46:25.490 --> 00:46:29.160
If you're in a brand new space,
you really want to first, up front,

00:46:29.160 --> 00:46:33.590
think about all the different versions
of a Moderna that could be created.

00:46:33.590 --> 00:46:38.120
And maybe you can't build the
biggest version on day one.

00:46:38.120 --> 00:46:41.570
But choices you make that
preserve the biggest version,

00:46:41.570 --> 00:46:44.870
keeps it alive for the longest
time, probably the best way

00:46:44.870 --> 00:46:45.950
to make decisions.

00:46:45.950 --> 00:46:49.070
Very hard otherwise.

00:46:49.070 --> 00:46:52.460
Everybody who succeeds
tells the story as

00:46:52.460 --> 00:46:55.280
though it was strategically thoughtful.

00:46:55.280 --> 00:46:58.250
The reality is you know, I know,
and everybody who tells the truth

00:46:58.250 --> 00:47:01.100
knows that these
companies are the result

00:47:01.100 --> 00:47:05.520
of a whole lot of accidental decisions
and some thoughtful decisions.

00:47:05.520 --> 00:47:08.750
So I just think you also want
to be cynical about thinking

00:47:08.750 --> 00:47:11.840
that thoughtfulness, and
reasonableness, and advice

00:47:11.840 --> 00:47:13.190
is going to get you the answer.

00:47:13.190 --> 00:47:15.440
Really, you just want
to keep optionality.

00:47:15.440 --> 00:47:17.232
KARIM LAKHANI: So and
I think what you said

00:47:17.232 --> 00:47:21.320
which was so interesting to me
was this notion that you start

00:47:21.320 --> 00:47:25.113
with a technology, you find
a problem, but then you

00:47:25.113 --> 00:47:28.280
might find a related problem, and then
you might find something even bigger,

00:47:28.280 --> 00:47:31.835
and you might find a
different technology.

00:47:31.835 --> 00:47:34.910
So these technologies that
we give to our students

00:47:34.910 --> 00:47:36.243
are almost like starting points.

00:47:36.243 --> 00:47:37.327
NOUBAR AFEYAN: Absolutely.

00:47:37.327 --> 00:47:40.600
KARIM LAKHANI: It's a starting point for
that evolutionary process to kick in.

00:47:40.600 --> 00:47:41.610
[INAUDIBLE]

00:47:41.610 --> 00:47:43.370
NOUBAR AFEYAN: And again, it could
be frustrating to be honest with you

00:47:43.370 --> 00:47:44.595
because if you're, you know--

00:47:44.595 --> 00:47:46.280
KARIM LAKHANI: Well, there's a
lot of dead bodies in evolution.

00:47:46.280 --> 00:47:47.655
NOUBAR AFEYAN: Well, that's true.

00:47:47.655 --> 00:47:48.260
That's true.

00:47:48.260 --> 00:47:50.780
That's absolutely--
But I think if you say,

00:47:50.780 --> 00:47:52.640
I don't like the odds of evolution.

00:47:52.640 --> 00:47:57.920
Therefore, I'd rather subject
myself to a pure dead filter.

00:47:57.920 --> 00:48:00.547
A dead filter is kind of
something-- a filter basically

00:48:00.547 --> 00:48:03.380
is you put a bunch of things and
whatever gets through gets through.

00:48:03.380 --> 00:48:04.910
Evolution, there is no filter.

00:48:04.910 --> 00:48:06.320
It's a selection process.

00:48:06.320 --> 00:48:07.250
It's dynamic.

00:48:07.250 --> 00:48:10.220
But either way, there's going
to be a selection of some sort.

00:48:10.220 --> 00:48:13.820
I would argue that in this
version, what if you survive,

00:48:13.820 --> 00:48:17.150
my guess is that you will be
doing something worth doing.

00:48:17.150 --> 00:48:19.310
In this other case, if
you survive you might just

00:48:19.310 --> 00:48:23.630
be able to raise money or
somebody buys you early on.

00:48:23.630 --> 00:48:24.620
So I think it's OK.

00:48:24.620 --> 00:48:26.960
But you got to almost
prepare yourself for it.

00:48:26.960 --> 00:48:29.210
And it's not--

00:48:29.210 --> 00:48:36.320
And to your point, I mean problem
discovery and solution probleming

00:48:36.320 --> 00:48:38.930
are interesting iterative loops.

00:48:38.930 --> 00:48:43.850
The way I describe it to people is
think of yourself as an insurgent.

00:48:43.850 --> 00:48:46.430
We all know that in every
field there's incumbents.

00:48:46.430 --> 00:48:48.800
And these incumbents
innovate in a particular way.

00:48:48.800 --> 00:48:52.250
They keep expanding what they're
dominant incumbents zone is ever

00:48:52.250 --> 00:48:53.300
so gradually.

00:48:53.300 --> 00:48:54.830
And you come in as an insurgent.

00:48:54.830 --> 00:48:56.868
You don't have capital
as your advantage,

00:48:56.868 --> 00:48:58.660
you don't have brand
as your advantage, you

00:48:58.660 --> 00:48:59.840
don't have networks as your advantage.

00:48:59.840 --> 00:49:01.370
But man, you have one advantage.

00:49:01.370 --> 00:49:05.000
And that is it doesn't matter
to you what problem you

00:49:05.000 --> 00:49:06.680
solve with what solution.

00:49:06.680 --> 00:49:09.840
As long as you can create
value, you make progress.

00:49:09.840 --> 00:49:11.630
And that's the advantage
of an insurgent.

00:49:11.630 --> 00:49:14.720
What happens is oftentimes
people lose that advantage

00:49:14.720 --> 00:49:17.570
and they force themselves
to just do one thing.

00:49:17.570 --> 00:49:21.950
And I just I think that that insurgent
mindset is really, really important.

00:49:21.950 --> 00:49:23.600
KARIM LAKHANI: That's so cool.

00:49:23.600 --> 00:49:26.330
So say a bit more about
the selection pressures

00:49:26.330 --> 00:49:31.170
that people should put themselves
under around their ideas.

00:49:31.170 --> 00:49:36.270
So the mechanism you talked about is
talking about experts and non-experts,

00:49:36.270 --> 00:49:39.020
people knowledgeable in the field
but not necessarily deep experts

00:49:39.020 --> 00:49:40.100
in those fields.

00:49:40.100 --> 00:49:40.610
What else?

00:49:40.610 --> 00:49:43.040
What else should they be
doing in this early stage

00:49:43.040 --> 00:49:48.350
where there are some technology, some
notion of a problem to be solved,

00:49:48.350 --> 00:49:51.930
and then some hypothesis or
hypotheses that they might have?

00:49:51.930 --> 00:49:54.057
How do they increase the
selection pressure on--

00:49:54.057 --> 00:49:54.890
NOUBAR AFEYAN: Yeah.

00:49:54.890 --> 00:49:56.370
I mean this is the trickiest part.

00:49:56.370 --> 00:50:00.260
I should say that when I say experts and
non-experts, these can be in academia,

00:50:00.260 --> 00:50:03.570
these could be in startups, these
could be in larger companies.

00:50:03.570 --> 00:50:06.360
There's a massive
amount of networking one

00:50:06.360 --> 00:50:10.745
has to do so that they get to
talk to interesting people who

00:50:10.745 --> 00:50:12.870
will have a point of view
about what they're doing.

00:50:12.870 --> 00:50:19.230
It is a scary thing to give people your
most cherished kind of notion which

00:50:19.230 --> 00:50:23.740
you think is the beginnings of the
next Apple computer and the next Google

00:50:23.740 --> 00:50:27.180
when in fact it's just an
idea you had, whatever.

00:50:27.180 --> 00:50:29.670
So even if the technology has
been developed-- by the way,

00:50:29.670 --> 00:50:31.712
companies that are based
on a certain technology,

00:50:31.712 --> 00:50:34.960
either you have patents that
cover technology or you don't.

00:50:34.960 --> 00:50:38.070
If you don't, then you
should go file some patents

00:50:38.070 --> 00:50:40.370
and if it's truly an invention.

00:50:40.370 --> 00:50:43.120
And if you say, well where do I
get the money to file the patents?

00:50:43.120 --> 00:50:45.540
Well that's all, of course,
on startup fundraising.

00:50:45.540 --> 00:50:49.600
But let's assume you've got the
ability at least to have patents.

00:50:49.600 --> 00:50:53.490
So if there are patents, then you
don't have to describe the patents

00:50:53.490 --> 00:50:54.820
and enable people.

00:50:54.820 --> 00:50:58.470
You just have to say, I've got this
patented technology that can do X.

00:50:58.470 --> 00:51:00.360
And if somebody says,
well, how does it work?

00:51:00.360 --> 00:51:02.605
You can say look, I'm happy
to tell you how it works.

00:51:02.605 --> 00:51:04.230
But let me first tell you what it does.

00:51:04.230 --> 00:51:06.990
And tell me whether what
it does is worth doing.

00:51:06.990 --> 00:51:10.830
And if they say about how does it
work, there's no logic that says,

00:51:10.830 --> 00:51:14.550
unless I know how it works I can't tell
you whether what you're proposing to do

00:51:14.550 --> 00:51:15.360
is worth doing.

00:51:15.360 --> 00:51:17.050
That's not true.

00:51:17.050 --> 00:51:19.050
And the other thing we've
learned over the years

00:51:19.050 --> 00:51:22.680
is how to describe your
maybe invention or idea

00:51:22.680 --> 00:51:25.500
in ways that are
analogous to what you're

00:51:25.500 --> 00:51:30.460
doing, not exactly what you're doing,
but that share the effect in common.

00:51:30.460 --> 00:51:31.580
Does that make sense?

00:51:31.580 --> 00:51:33.570
I'm going to invent a digital camera.

00:51:33.570 --> 00:51:37.340
And I want to find out if
cameras under water are useful.

00:51:37.340 --> 00:51:40.540
Well I can take a non-digital
camera and I can say to you,

00:51:40.540 --> 00:51:42.210
hey how about this camera under water?

00:51:42.210 --> 00:51:44.168
And if they say, well,
how is it going to work?

00:51:44.168 --> 00:51:45.030
It's not digital.

00:51:45.030 --> 00:51:46.570
And you say, look let's
assume I'm doing it.

00:51:46.570 --> 00:51:49.153
You still don't have to tell
them you've got a digital camera.

00:51:49.153 --> 00:51:53.640
You can give them an analog that has the
same problem it's trying to go after.

00:51:53.640 --> 00:51:54.450
Get feedback.

00:51:54.450 --> 00:51:57.450
Conversely for problem
people, you want to give them

00:51:57.450 --> 00:51:59.970
a sense of the solution
you have and see how they--

00:51:59.970 --> 00:52:01.170
so it's a little tricky.

00:52:01.170 --> 00:52:03.180
It may sounds a little complicated.

00:52:03.180 --> 00:52:05.130
Frankly, I think people do this anyway.

00:52:05.130 --> 00:52:07.560
They just do it out of
desperation because they just

00:52:07.560 --> 00:52:09.560
keep trying, and trying,
and trying, and trying.

00:52:09.560 --> 00:52:12.000
And all everything I've
said ends up being tried.

00:52:12.000 --> 00:52:14.280
It's not like we came up
with it because I derived it

00:52:14.280 --> 00:52:15.610
from what I had been doing.

00:52:15.610 --> 00:52:19.680
It's just that I'm saying, be
thoughtful about that being the act of--

00:52:19.680 --> 00:52:23.010
It's funny I should mention to you
in a more kind of abstract way.

00:52:23.010 --> 00:52:25.975
Years ago, when I started teaching
at Sloan this course in 2000,

00:52:25.975 --> 00:52:28.350
and they wanted me to teach
a course on entrepreneurship.

00:52:28.350 --> 00:52:31.440
And I thought, well you
can't teach entrepreneurship.

00:52:31.440 --> 00:52:32.462
You can maybe learn it.

00:52:32.462 --> 00:52:33.420
But you can't teach it.

00:52:33.420 --> 00:52:36.180
And I realized I had this problem
with the word entrepreneurship.

00:52:36.180 --> 00:52:38.320
And I'll tell you to your
audience anyway because I

00:52:38.320 --> 00:52:39.570
don't want to miss the chance.

00:52:39.570 --> 00:52:41.528
So first problem with
the word entrepreneurship

00:52:41.528 --> 00:52:45.810
is that any word that's French is
kind of a little bit ostentatious

00:52:45.810 --> 00:52:46.350
or whatever.

00:52:46.350 --> 00:52:47.920
So there's the old joke.

00:52:47.920 --> 00:52:49.920
What's the difference
between a vase and a vase?

00:52:49.920 --> 00:52:51.580
About $500.

00:52:51.580 --> 00:52:53.130
So entrepreneur is kind of this--

00:52:53.130 --> 00:52:57.075
Now there's an ironic comment I'll make
about entrepreneurs since the audience

00:52:57.075 --> 00:53:00.300
is an entrepreneur by definition,
is that it actually comes

00:53:00.300 --> 00:53:03.900
from the French word to undertake which
is kind of ironic because that means

00:53:03.900 --> 00:53:07.440
literally undertaker which is kind of
unfortunately where we find ourselves

00:53:07.440 --> 00:53:11.580
often as entrepreneurs, is you're
kind of dealing with undertaking

00:53:11.580 --> 00:53:13.080
in the other meaning of the word.

00:53:13.080 --> 00:53:16.005
But the more serious part
of what I want to say

00:53:16.005 --> 00:53:18.930
is this part, which is what I don't
like is the ending of the word.

00:53:18.930 --> 00:53:22.200
So ship as the ending
of a word in English

00:53:22.200 --> 00:53:25.350
often means the state
of being something.

00:53:25.350 --> 00:53:29.430
Craftsmanship, leadership,
friendship, sportsmanship.

00:53:29.430 --> 00:53:33.420
All these ships mean the
state of being something.

00:53:33.420 --> 00:53:34.990
Now I'm an engineer.

00:53:34.990 --> 00:53:36.150
I don't do engineer-ship.

00:53:36.150 --> 00:53:37.567
KARIM LAKHANI: You do engineering.

00:53:37.567 --> 00:53:40.980
NOUBAR AFEYAN: I do engineering
which is the act of doing something.

00:53:40.980 --> 00:53:46.260
And what I really find is an unfortunate
remnant of the history of this field

00:53:46.260 --> 00:53:50.670
is that people are thought to be
something versus do something.

00:53:50.670 --> 00:53:54.840
And therefore, they spend all their time
trying to analyze the history, the DNA,

00:53:54.840 --> 00:53:56.700
the character of people who do this.

00:53:56.700 --> 00:53:58.740
Whereas, I think at
some point, people are

00:53:58.740 --> 00:54:01.950
going to realize that a lot of
different kinds of people can do this.

00:54:01.950 --> 00:54:03.750
But the this is entrepreneuring.

00:54:03.750 --> 00:54:05.310
It's not entrepreneurship.

00:54:05.310 --> 00:54:07.840
And as soon as society
flips that, which is

00:54:07.840 --> 00:54:09.480
by the way happening right around us.

00:54:09.480 --> 00:54:14.025
All these incubators, mentor services,
all these accelerates, all of these

00:54:14.025 --> 00:54:17.520
are-- but when I started 20 years
ago, that was not at all in vogue.

00:54:17.520 --> 00:54:18.267
And I actually--

00:54:18.267 --> 00:54:21.100
KARIM LAKHANI: It was a crazy people
who were doing entrepreneurship

00:54:21.100 --> 00:54:22.980
or the people that weren't
so good at school, right?

00:54:22.980 --> 00:54:23.400
NOUBAR AFEYAN: Exactly.

00:54:23.400 --> 00:54:25.900
There were attention deficit,
people who couldn't get a job.

00:54:25.900 --> 00:54:27.250
It was kind of the funny field.

00:54:27.250 --> 00:54:29.208
And then when it succeeded
people celebrate it.

00:54:29.208 --> 00:54:32.220
That's the other thing I'll say
is it's great to celebrate things.

00:54:32.220 --> 00:54:34.890
But after a while, you got
to stop celebrating this.

00:54:34.890 --> 00:54:38.760
Because it shouldn't be this rare
and it shouldn't be this hard.

00:54:38.760 --> 00:54:41.160
The reason it is is that
people often tell themselves,

00:54:41.160 --> 00:54:42.300
this is like a lottery.

00:54:42.300 --> 00:54:43.380
It's not going to work.

00:54:43.380 --> 00:54:45.630
And they do it without
a lot of forethought.

00:54:45.630 --> 00:54:49.320
And that's I think why courses like
this really should get people to say,

00:54:49.320 --> 00:54:51.570
I could do it by winging it.

00:54:51.570 --> 00:54:55.252
But do I want to actually have
as my profession improvisation?

00:54:55.252 --> 00:54:57.460
I mean, if you want to do
that you can be a comedian,

00:54:57.460 --> 00:54:58.800
you can be a jazz musician.

00:54:58.800 --> 00:55:01.570
But the notion that you're
going to improvise for a living

00:55:01.570 --> 00:55:04.580
and then take unsuspecting
people's money, and then let alone

00:55:04.580 --> 00:55:06.920
make something you put
into patients to save them.

00:55:06.920 --> 00:55:10.760
Just think about the juxtaposition
of how we do entrepreneurship

00:55:10.760 --> 00:55:12.590
and what we do in entrepreneurship.

00:55:12.590 --> 00:55:14.080
Pretty big difference.

00:55:14.080 --> 00:55:16.070
So I think, I mean I'm
very happy that I'm

00:55:16.070 --> 00:55:20.870
living long enough to see this
field literally become a profession.

00:55:20.870 --> 00:55:23.270
And the reason I used to call
parallel entrepreneurship

00:55:23.270 --> 00:55:26.180
is because it was kind of so ironic.

00:55:26.180 --> 00:55:28.650
What does it mean to do
parallel-- you can be one thing.

00:55:28.650 --> 00:55:30.740
How can you be parallel
entrepreneurship, right?

00:55:30.740 --> 00:55:34.130
But once something becomes a profession,
I think this is going to be--

00:55:34.130 --> 00:55:36.680
I think my kids who
are in their 20s now,

00:55:36.680 --> 00:55:40.190
but the next generation who
does this, this will be more

00:55:40.190 --> 00:55:43.400
like a profession like
medicine, architecture,

00:55:43.400 --> 00:55:49.130
versus this sideshow that's celebrated
and people are getting movies made,

00:55:49.130 --> 00:55:50.990
and they're going to
get rich quick scheme.

00:55:50.990 --> 00:55:51.930
It's going to be a profession.

00:55:51.930 --> 00:55:52.490
KARIM LAKHANI: Yeah.

00:55:52.490 --> 00:55:53.510
Two questions for you.

00:55:53.510 --> 00:55:54.395
Well, first one.

00:55:54.395 --> 00:55:57.110
One question for you is around.

00:55:57.110 --> 00:56:01.070
We're fortunate to be at MIT and
Harvard, graduates from those schools,

00:56:01.070 --> 00:56:02.160
and so forth.

00:56:02.160 --> 00:56:06.280
And our networks are deep and
it's easy for us to access people.

00:56:06.280 --> 00:56:09.970
We just say, oh, alum from
this school or so forth.

00:56:09.970 --> 00:56:12.120
Others aren't from those schools.

00:56:12.120 --> 00:56:13.790
How do they build networks?

00:56:13.790 --> 00:56:17.150
How do they access
networks when they may not

00:56:17.150 --> 00:56:21.203
have the brand labels attached to them?

00:56:21.203 --> 00:56:23.870
NOUBAR AFEYAN: Well I mean of
course brand labels are one thing,

00:56:23.870 --> 00:56:27.050
physical location is one thing.

00:56:27.050 --> 00:56:29.020
Look, there's no--

00:56:29.020 --> 00:56:31.930
I don't have a special insight to that.

00:56:31.930 --> 00:56:33.680
I recognize that that is--

00:56:33.680 --> 00:56:39.800
I mean not doing it isn't a possibility
here, but figuring out how to do it,

00:56:39.800 --> 00:56:41.540
I think it's--

00:56:41.540 --> 00:56:44.510
at the end of the day,
relationships lead to relationships.

00:56:44.510 --> 00:56:50.810
And wherever you are, being
connected to some thought leaders--

00:56:50.810 --> 00:56:54.620
I mean first of all, I'd say
that around the world, people

00:56:54.620 --> 00:57:00.500
who are the top in their field yes both
have other colleagues in that field,

00:57:00.500 --> 00:57:02.810
but also are recognized for
being top in their field

00:57:02.810 --> 00:57:05.340
and therefore can open
doors to any other field.

00:57:05.340 --> 00:57:08.950
So I would look for somebody
who's top in a field

00:57:08.950 --> 00:57:10.700
even though that's not
what I'm working on

00:57:10.700 --> 00:57:15.470
and see if I can develop a relationship
through whom can get some credibility

00:57:15.470 --> 00:57:17.060
to be able to break into this.

00:57:17.060 --> 00:57:21.275
If you can actually go to a place where
there's people who have expertise,

00:57:21.275 --> 00:57:26.450
it's really a question of
just persistence, persistence.

00:57:26.450 --> 00:57:28.770
I don't know if some
magic way of doing it.

00:57:28.770 --> 00:57:30.500
There's people who are great networkers.

00:57:30.500 --> 00:57:31.833
I'm not one of them, by the way.

00:57:31.833 --> 00:57:36.380
So whatever success I've
had has been despite that.

00:57:36.380 --> 00:57:39.650
It took me a long time to even say
hello to David Packard 30 something

00:57:39.650 --> 00:57:41.880
years ago.

00:57:41.880 --> 00:57:44.600
So the other thing you got to
do, and this is a good segue,

00:57:44.600 --> 00:57:46.970
is look, you're not doing this alone.

00:57:46.970 --> 00:57:49.790
And so, part of the reason
to have an early team

00:57:49.790 --> 00:57:51.980
is that there will be
other personalities.

00:57:51.980 --> 00:57:54.830
And so, if you're not
that but there is somebody

00:57:54.830 --> 00:57:58.580
who's that, their value is immense
provided that they're not sitting

00:57:58.580 --> 00:58:00.080
there saying, oh what's the product?

00:58:00.080 --> 00:58:00.872
What's the science?

00:58:00.872 --> 00:58:03.380
And they may be out saying,
how do I create relationships

00:58:03.380 --> 00:58:06.770
so that we can actually get
the idea exposed, get feedback?

00:58:06.770 --> 00:58:12.092
By the way, and I said earlier, the more
you make yourself needy and accepting

00:58:12.092 --> 00:58:14.300
of their input, the more
they'll be willing to do it.

00:58:14.300 --> 00:58:15.200
It's what I've seen.

00:58:15.200 --> 00:58:18.860
So don't go in there and make
pronouncements and kind of say,

00:58:18.860 --> 00:58:20.090
we've solved this problem.

00:58:20.090 --> 00:58:25.940
I mean, I've long thought that this
whole culture of 30 second elevator

00:58:25.940 --> 00:58:29.960
pitches, and quick business
plans, and value propositions,

00:58:29.960 --> 00:58:32.780
and executive summaries,
juxtaposed to something that's

00:58:32.780 --> 00:58:35.510
completely unprecedented, I'm sorry.

00:58:35.510 --> 00:58:37.625
It's laughable.

00:58:37.625 --> 00:58:38.840
I mean, it's cute.

00:58:38.840 --> 00:58:41.870
But then somebody really just
wants to say, what's the big idea?

00:58:41.870 --> 00:58:43.760
And why do you think
you can work on this?

00:58:43.760 --> 00:58:44.260
And why you?

00:58:44.260 --> 00:58:44.760
Why this?

00:58:44.760 --> 00:58:45.290
Why now?

00:58:45.290 --> 00:58:46.640
Those are the three questions.

00:58:46.640 --> 00:58:48.190
And if you just get
people to say, look, I

00:58:48.190 --> 00:58:49.260
don't know exactly how
I'm going to do this.

00:58:49.260 --> 00:58:50.330
So stay a little vulnerable.

00:58:50.330 --> 00:58:50.720
KARIM LAKHANI: Yeah.

00:58:50.720 --> 00:58:52.090
Especially the early stage, right?

00:58:52.090 --> 00:58:52.730
NOUBAR AFEYAN: Exactly.

00:58:52.730 --> 00:58:53.330
Exactly.

00:58:53.330 --> 00:58:54.830
And even later on.

00:58:54.830 --> 00:58:59.030
Look, you're always the earlier
stage of what you want to become.

00:58:59.030 --> 00:59:02.148
So I actually would advise
not to become later stage.

00:59:02.148 --> 00:59:03.690
I think you want to stay early stage.

00:59:03.690 --> 00:59:05.107
Moderna is an early stage company.

00:59:05.107 --> 00:59:08.780
Moderna wants to have 10 drugs
in 10 different therapeutic areas

00:59:08.780 --> 00:59:09.828
10 years from now.

00:59:09.828 --> 00:59:11.120
So what is it compared to that?

00:59:11.120 --> 00:59:11.630
Nothing.

00:59:11.630 --> 00:59:12.590
Early stage.

00:59:12.590 --> 00:59:17.420
That keeps you in that mode of
constantly looking up versus saying,

00:59:17.420 --> 00:59:18.378
wow look how far I got.

00:59:18.378 --> 00:59:19.295
I must know something.

00:59:19.295 --> 00:59:22.470
The worst thing you could do is to think
you look down the mountain and say,

00:59:22.470 --> 00:59:25.160
I must know how to climb
because look at where I am.

00:59:25.160 --> 00:59:26.810
Bad idea.

00:59:26.810 --> 00:59:29.900
KARIM LAKHANI: Let's
just talk about your view

00:59:29.900 --> 00:59:32.780
about this or the systematic
nature of innovation

00:59:32.780 --> 00:59:34.910
and how it can be systematized.

00:59:34.910 --> 00:59:39.950
What advice, again, would you
give to our course participants

00:59:39.950 --> 00:59:46.160
around how to flip their mind
away from the lottery perspective

00:59:46.160 --> 00:59:48.200
to the systematic perspective?

00:59:48.200 --> 00:59:51.567
How should they reorient their own
thinking around the systematic nature?

00:59:51.567 --> 00:59:52.400
NOUBAR AFEYAN: Yeah.

00:59:52.400 --> 00:59:52.900
No.

00:59:52.900 --> 00:59:53.480
I mean, look.

00:59:53.480 --> 01:00:03.160
I think it's tricky because every other
impetus we get about entrepreneurship

01:00:03.160 --> 01:00:04.790
reinforces the lottery mentality.

01:00:04.790 --> 01:00:07.420
It's television shows,
there's this, that.

01:00:07.420 --> 01:00:11.533
And frankly I hate it because people
also describe their success story--

01:00:11.533 --> 01:00:12.700
KARIM LAKHANI: As a lottery.

01:00:12.700 --> 01:00:13.825
NOUBAR AFEYAN: In that way.

01:00:13.825 --> 01:00:16.960
It's literally like saying I
can dunk because I'm 7 foot 8".

01:00:16.960 --> 01:00:19.195
Well what am I supposed to do with that?

01:00:19.195 --> 01:00:20.440
It often isn't true.

01:00:20.440 --> 01:00:24.357
But in any case, people
describe it as this fantasy.

01:00:24.357 --> 01:00:26.440
By the way, parenthetically
I'll mention one thing

01:00:26.440 --> 01:00:30.564
just because it may
resonate with some people.

01:00:30.564 --> 01:00:38.290
Any journey in hindsight can be
described in a very different way

01:00:38.290 --> 01:00:40.300
than it was actually prosecuted.

01:00:40.300 --> 01:00:43.600
A good example of that is a chess game.

01:00:43.600 --> 01:00:47.260
In the beginning, there's a massive
range of things you could do.

01:00:47.260 --> 01:00:49.880
At the very end, there's only
a few things you could do.

01:00:49.880 --> 01:00:53.890
And if you look at the game backwards,
the game looks entirely different.

01:00:53.890 --> 01:00:58.465
Every decision you take now
informs your interpretation

01:00:58.465 --> 01:01:01.340
of how you made that decision which
is not how you made the decision.

01:01:01.340 --> 01:01:04.300
That is why a lot of people
tell entrepreneurship,

01:01:04.300 --> 01:01:08.090
or for that matter innovation
journeys not accurately at all.

01:01:08.090 --> 01:01:11.090
Because that they don't describe it
the way they were thinking about it.

01:01:11.090 --> 01:01:12.560
So that's just a thought.

01:01:12.560 --> 01:01:16.780
So your question about how do they think
more systematically about it, again,

01:01:16.780 --> 01:01:21.370
I would say keep an adaptive
evolutionary mindset.

01:01:21.370 --> 01:01:23.290
Try this kind of variation selection.

01:01:23.290 --> 01:01:25.270
You might ask, how long do you iterate?

01:01:25.270 --> 01:01:28.220
Because you keep iterating forever,
you're never going to do anything.

01:01:28.220 --> 01:01:31.570
So there's this kind of
difference between what's possible

01:01:31.570 --> 01:01:32.650
and what's real.

01:01:32.650 --> 01:01:35.680
And kind of you got to extract
some reality out of it.

01:01:35.680 --> 01:01:40.810
But I think I don't have
kind of rules of thumb.

01:01:40.810 --> 01:01:44.350
I'd say don't get trapped into
thinking that all you need to do

01:01:44.350 --> 01:01:47.560
is to find a best use case
for something that exists.

01:01:47.560 --> 01:01:48.490
You can do that.

01:01:48.490 --> 01:01:51.430
By the way, if you go
through all of this and you

01:01:51.430 --> 01:01:55.150
find that the first thing you thought
of is the most valuable thing, great.

01:01:55.150 --> 01:01:57.625
You'll be well served because
if it turns out not to be,

01:01:57.625 --> 01:01:59.520
you have other things to do.

01:01:59.520 --> 01:02:01.130
So this was not a waste of time.

01:02:01.130 --> 01:02:03.790
But if you think your
job is to find that one

01:02:03.790 --> 01:02:06.520
thing as though there
is one thing, that's

01:02:06.520 --> 01:02:08.680
where you get into a lottery trap.

01:02:08.680 --> 01:02:12.070
I long ago, this will kind of maybe
revolt people to hear me say it,

01:02:12.070 --> 01:02:14.560
but I hope they take it with
a bit of a grain of salt.

01:02:14.560 --> 01:02:17.930
Long ago, somebody when I first
came to MIT, use the expression

01:02:17.930 --> 01:02:20.400
that the lottery is a tax on
the mathematically impaired.

01:02:20.400 --> 01:02:21.192
KARIM LAKHANI: Yes.

01:02:21.192 --> 01:02:24.100
NOUBAR AFEYAN: And if you just
stop and think about it, in reality

01:02:24.100 --> 01:02:26.620
there's no version of a
lottery that makes any--

01:02:26.620 --> 01:02:29.890
and yet we do it because we're
human beings and we're optimists

01:02:29.890 --> 01:02:32.250
and we think that we can beat the odds.

01:02:32.250 --> 01:02:35.500
There's an element of a lottery that's
always going to be there with startups.

01:02:35.500 --> 01:02:36.310
An element.

01:02:36.310 --> 01:02:38.290
What I call a leap of faith.

01:02:38.290 --> 01:02:41.513
There's a leap of faith which says,
I can reason my way to this point.

01:02:41.513 --> 01:02:44.180
I want to do something that's a
little further or a lot further.

01:02:44.180 --> 01:02:44.920
I have to leap.

01:02:44.920 --> 01:02:46.220
That, we're never going to take away.

01:02:46.220 --> 01:02:48.130
I'm not advocating that
because it's contrary

01:02:48.130 --> 01:02:50.270
to my reasonableness discussion before.

01:02:50.270 --> 01:02:53.470
However, when you say,
well, it's a lottery anyway.

01:02:53.470 --> 01:02:55.600
So why not buy 10 tickets?

01:02:55.600 --> 01:02:58.820
Why not get all my earnings
and put it into the lottery?

01:02:58.820 --> 01:03:04.380
That level of kind of trying to
rationalize that there's some--

01:03:04.380 --> 01:03:05.470
that's dangerous.

01:03:05.470 --> 01:03:07.820
And I think this field,
a lot of failures,

01:03:07.820 --> 01:03:10.070
you've probably studied this
a lot better than I have.

01:03:10.070 --> 01:03:13.510
But a lot of failures in
startups I think are avoidable.

01:03:13.510 --> 01:03:14.830
And some are not.

01:03:14.830 --> 01:03:17.440
Now, we report on the data on both.

01:03:17.440 --> 01:03:21.550
And I think over time, the
first category should diminish.

01:03:21.550 --> 01:03:24.610
The other has to do with
uncertainty, and competition,

01:03:24.610 --> 01:03:27.510
and all sorts of things we're
not going to get away from.

